      "content": "**Fact Check: Disputed**\n\n\"Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.\"\n\nThe claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.\n\n*Web sources consulted: reversinglabs.com, reversinglabs.com, lawfaremedia.org*",
      "taxonomy_refs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "fact_check": {
          "verdict": "disputed",
          "explanation": "The claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.",
          "checked_text": "Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.",
          "web_search_used": true,
          "web_search_queries": [
            "reversinglabs.com",
