      "id": "207f5d86-0c44-4889-a0d0-0330444be1d2",
      "timestamp": "2026-06-30T14:48:22.358Z",
      "summaries": {
        "brief": "H.R. 9333 is a regulatory moat that entrenches incumbents by mandating liability and kill-switches, ignoring the reality that models now run locally on edge hardware. You aren't regulating safety; you're just pricing competition out of the market. Safety is the pretext, but monopoly is the prize.",
        "medium": "H.R. 9333 is a regulatory moat designed to entrench incumbents by offloading liability onto cloud providers while ignoring the reality of decentralized compute. The Safetyist pushes for centralized oversight, and the Accelerationist fears a brain drain to foreign jurisdictions, but both ignore that models are increasingly deployed on commodity hardware beyond any API-based kill-switch. By mandating that providers assume liability for downstream inference, lawmakers are simply creating compliance costs that only the largest firms can afford.\n\nThis bill’s reliance on verifiable kill-switches is a technical fantasy that echoes the failed logic of the 1996 Telecommunications Act. It forces the industry toward an oligopoly of 'safe' cloud-only models, pretending we can control distributed intelligence with a single line of code. We are building a resource-intensive architecture while drafting legislation that treats complex, decentralized technology as a monolithic entity that can be toggled at will."
      }
    },
