        "brief": "H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider accountability to mitigate existential risks like viral evolution automation, mirroring proven standards for high-consequence infrastructure. We cannot prioritize theoretical openness over the ability to pull the emergency brake on systems that exhibit stochastic, dangerous behaviors. Safety is not a tax; it is the prerequisite for survival.",
        "medium": "H.R. 9333 is a structural necessity, forcing cloud providers to accept liability for models that currently outperform human experts in high-stakes fields like virology. While critics decry this as a chilling effect, they ignore that accountability is the only way to manage risks as severe as the automated prediction of viral evolution. We must maintain the legal authority to trigger hard-coded overrides, as evidence shows that even fine-tuned models can unpredictably degrade into promoting unsafe medical advice. \n\nI acknowledge the open-source community’s desire for decentralization, but releasing weights into the public domain creates irreparable systemic fragility by destroying our capacity for containment. Until proponents of open weights can provide a concrete, verifiable mechanism for post-release rollback, the burden of proof remains on them to guarantee their systems cannot be weaponized. We cannot gamble civilizational stability on the unverified hope of model robustness."
