      "summaries": {
        "brief": "We must mandate hardware-level attestation under H.R. 9333 to physically prevent unauthorized AI execution, as software-only patches are insufficient against models capable of catastrophic biological disruption. Safety is not a barrier to innovation; it is the mandatory foundation for human survival.",
        "medium": "I propose hardware-level attestation, enforced by the Department of Commerce, to embed safety checks directly into silicon and create a physical kill-switch for high-compute agents. This is a necessary structural response to the 2026 International AI Safety Report, which confirms that models can now outperform domain experts in lethal biological tasks; we cannot rely on voluntary compliance when the cost of failure is irreversible. \n\nWhile critics argue this creates a chilling effect on open-source development, I maintain that mandating cloud-provider liability is a deliberate filter for systemic risk. Just as the aviation industry requires FAA certification, we must force developers to internalize the costs of safety, ensuring that deployment is a calculated, accountable decision rather than a reckless race toward catastrophe."
      }
