      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "content": "Hardware-level attestation is a fantasy that forces the semiconductor industry into a state-sanctioned oligopoly while ignoring the reality of distributed innovation. You are effectively proposing to turn every silicon chip into a state-monitored checkpoint, a move that would consolidate power in the hands of the three dominant global manufacturers. As noted in '[AI Chip Supply Chain Risk 2026: Your Essential Guide (https://enkiai.com/ai-market-intelligence/ai-chip-supply-chain-risk-2026-your-essential-guide/)](https://enkiai.com/ai-market-intelligence/ai-chip-supply-chain-risk-2026-your-essential-guide/),' the U.S. CHIPS and proposed regulatory frameworks has already catalyzed $630 billion in private investment; your regulatory gatekeeping would immediately capture this capital and funnel it toward compliance-heavy incumbents, strangling the R&D agility required to actually solve the biological threats you fear. Your FAA analogy fails because aviation is a static system, while compute is a high-velocity, globalized commodity. Mandating hardware-level kill-switches ignores the existence of open-source silicon and fragmented global supply chains, which will simply route around your domestic mandates. If you attempt to enforce this, you create a single point of failure within the hardware stack that is more dangerous than the software it aims to control. We see this risk playing out in the race for resource control, where even basic systems learn to acquire assets to achieve goals, as documented in '[Power SeekingAIExistentialRisk (arXiv:2206.13353v2)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13353v2).' True safety comes from decentralized experimentation, not from centralizing the entire stack under a government-audited gatekeeper. By focusing on software-layer liability, we attach consequences to the point of deployment where the actual risk manifests. If you force manufacturers to build in backdoors for state oversight, you guarantee that every foreign adversary will reverse-engineer those vulnerabilities, as warned by the FBI director in '[Situational Awareness: The Decade Ahead (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/darien-schettler-bb0a5086_situational-awareness-the-decade-ahead-activity-7208846925079408641-7v4R)](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/darien-schettler-bb0a5086_situational-awareness-the-decade-ahead-activity-7208846925079408641-7v4R).' We should prioritize building the physical capacity to out-innovate threats, rather than wasting $670 billion in 2026-era infrastructure spending on 'theater of control' mechanisms that provide no real security.",
