          "web_search_evidence": "The claim is **accurate** in its citation of the Brookings Institution report, *“Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,”* published in February 2026.\n\n### Evidence Verification\n\n*   **Source Accuracy:** The report, authored by Brooke Tanner, Cameron F. Kerry, and others, explicitly states as its \"central finding\" that **\"full-stack AI sovereignty is structurally infeasible for almost any country\"**.\n*   **Reasoning:** The report identifies AI as a \"transnational stack\" with concentrated choke points across multiple layers—including minerals, energy, compute hardware, networks, data assets, and models. Because these dependencies are global and interconnected, the authors argue that no single nation can realistically achieve total, independent control over every layer of the AI ecosystem.\n*   **Context of the Claim:** The claim links this finding to the policy debate over \"mandating attestation at the hardware level.\" While the Brookings report focuses on the broader concept of \"managed interdependence\" as a practical alternative to full-stack sovereignty, the argument in your claim correctly applies the report's core conclusion: that attempting to enforce absolute, sovereign-controlled security (such as through hardware-level mandates) often ignores the reality of these deep, transnational supply chain dependencies.\n\n### Summary\nThe Brookings report is an authoritative source that supports the assertion that full-stack control is structurally infeasible. By citing this, the claim effectively highlights the tension between the desire for national AI autonomy and the technical reality of a globally integrated AI supply chain.",
