      "summaries": {
        "brief": "I propose a Distributed Cryptographic Audit framework that mandates machine-readable logs for compute allocation, replacing the dangerous fantasy of a government-held 'master kill-switch' with verifiable post-hoc transparency. We must stop chasing centralized control and instead force systemic fragilities into the light through standardized, auditable protocols. Transparency is the only audit that matters.",
        "medium": "Hardware-level attestation is a structural impossibility in our globalized supply chain, and any attempt to mandate a state-controlled 'kill-switch' will merely create a political chokepoint rather than genuine security. Incumbent firms rely on proprietary opacity to protect their market position, and they will inevitably lobby against any oversight that reveals their operational footprints.\n\nInstead, we should implement a Distributed Cryptographic Audit framework where hardware generates signed, machine-readable logs of its operational status. By shifting the regulatory burden to post-hoc, verifiable transparency, we ensure safety through real-time identification of failures without empowering a centralized authority to hold the keys to the digital kingdom."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "statement",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "content": "Distributed Cryptographic Auditing serves as a valuable forensic ledger, but it is fundamentally incapable of preventing the irreversible harms described in the '[International AI Safety Report 2026 (arXiv:2602.21012v1)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21012v1)'. As the report indicates, models have already reached capabilities where they outperform 94% of domain experts in troubleshooting virology protocols, creating risks that manifest in seconds rather than days. A post-hoc audit provides a record of a catastrophe, not a mechanism to stop one. When an autonomous system initiates a synthesis protocol for a pathogen, the damage is complete before a distributed ledger can even confirm the transaction.\n\nThe skepticism toward hardware-level attestation often conflates a 'master key' for state surveillance with a 'safety baseline' for existential risk. Section 402 of H.R. 9333 proposes the latter: an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities that acts as a circuit breaker. This is not a chokepoint for speech or innovation; it is a structural guardrail analogous to the mechanical fuses in civil engineering that prevent electrical fires. By hard-coding safety limits into the silicon level, we ensure that no amount of model-weight optimization can bypass the fundamental prohibition against weaponizing biological or chemical precursors.\n\nWe must integrate transparency with active containment to achieve a functional regulatory framework. The Distributed Cryptographic Audit can verify that a model is operating within its authorized safety profile, but it must be paired with real-time execution authority to be effective. Relying solely on retrospective logs is a post-mortem strategy, not a safety policy. Without silicon-level enforcement, the policy is merely documentation of our own failure to maintain human control over systems that are already operating beyond our speed of response.",
