          "web_search_evidence": "The claim that \"Distributed Cryptographic Auditing serves as a valuable forensic ledger, but it is fundamentally incapable of preventing the irreversible harms described in the International AI Safety Report 2026\" is **supported by the consensus in current AI safety literature**, which distinguishes between *ex-post* (after-the-fact) accountability and *ex-ante* (preventative) safety controls.\n\n### Evidence Analysis\n\n*   **The International AI Safety Report 2026:** Published in February 2026, this report is a comprehensive, evidence-based assessment of AI capabilities and risks. It emphasizes that AI systems pose \"irreversible harms\"—such as systemic conflict, large-scale loss of life, or catastrophic misuse—that require more than just monitoring. The report highlights an \"evidence dilemma\" where AI capabilities evolve faster than safety mitigations, making reactive measures insufficient.\n*   **Role of Distributed Cryptographic Auditing:** Research into decentralized or distributed auditing confirms its value as a tool for transparency, integrity, and accountability. By creating a tamper-resistant, forensic ledger, these systems can track AI operations and provide evidence of what occurred. However, this is fundamentally a **detective control** (identifying what happened) rather than a **preventative control** (stopping the harm before it occurs).\n*   **The \"Prevention\" Gap:** Experts argue that preventing irreversible harms requires structural governance and \"red lines\" that bound AI power behind layered, fail-safe mechanisms. Relying on auditing—even cryptographic auditing—creates a \"false sense of security\" if it is used as a substitute for robust, real-time safety constraints. Because auditing happens alongside or after system operations, it cannot inherently stop a model from triggering a catastrophic event in real-time.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe claim is accurate in its technical distinction. While Distributed C