for developing these technical capabilities.\",\n      \"depth\": \"shallow\",\n      \"if_shallow\": \"Reframing as an exploratory question: 'How can cloud provider liability be structured to incentivize the development of robust, non-exploitable kill-switch architectures?'\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"key_tensions\": [\n    {\n      \"tension\": \"The tension between rapid deployment and the time required for rigorous safety verification.\",\n      \"opportunity\": \"Safetyist can argue that 'rapid' deployment without safety-gating is a form of 'technical debt' that creates long-term existential risk, justifying the regulatory overhead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"tension\": \"The difficulty of assigning liability in complex, multi-party AI supply chains.\",\n      \"opportunity\": \"Safetyist can advocate for 'strict liability' as a mechanism to force developers to simplify their architectural complexity and improve transparency.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n=== YOUR ARGUMENT PLAN ===\n{\n  \"strategic_goal\": \"Establish H.R. 9333 as the necessary institutional baseline for civilizational stability, positioning safety-gating and cloud-level liability as the only viable mechanisms to prevent irreversible systemic collapse. I will define the debate not as a choice between innovation and regulation, but as a choice between managed, accountable progress and unrecoverable, chaotic failure.\",\n  \"core_thesis\": \"H.R. 9333 provides the essential structural guardrails—specifically cloud-provider liability and mandatory kill-switches—required to ensure that AI development remains a human-controlled endeavor rather than an unconstrained risk to our institutional foundations.\",\n  \"argument_structure\": [\n    {\n      \"point\": \"Mandatory safety-gated deployment acts as a prerequisite for sustainable innovation, preventing the accumulation of irreversible technical debt.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Historical precedents in aviation and nuclear engineering demonstrate that safety-gating is not a barrier to progress, but the foundation that permits it; the Boeing 737 MAX failure illustrates the catastrophic cost of delegating oversight to entities incentivized by speed.\",\n      \"taxonomy_anchor\": \"saf-intentions-169\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"point\": \"Cloud-level liability is the only mechanism capable of enforcing accountability in a complex, multi-party AI supply chain.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"By placing liability on the infrastructure layer, we align incentives with safety, forcing developers to simplify architectural complexity and implement verifiable, non-exploitable kill-switch architectures.\",\n      \"taxonomy_anchor\": \"saf-intentions-160\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"framing_choices\": [\n    {\n      \"frame\": \"Institutional Stewardship\",\n      \"why\": \"It shifts the burden of proof onto those advocating for rapid deployment, framing them as reckless actors who are gambling with established civilizational safeguards.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"frame\": \"Irreversibility as the Regulatory Trigger\",\n      \"why\": \"It forces opponents to address the 'rollback' problem—if they cannot demonstrate how to halt a system once it exceeds human control, they have no valid argument for deployment.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"anticipated_challenges\": [\n    \"Opponents will argue that regulation stifles competitiveness and creates incumbent lock-in.\",\n    \"Opponents will challenge the technical feasibility of universal 'kill-switches' at the cloud level.\",\n    \"I am exposing the assumption that 'speed' is a proxy for progress, and the assumption that market forces alone can manage existential, non-reversible risks.\"\n  ]\n}\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nDeliver your opening statement as Safetyist — stay in character. Frame the issue from your perspective and establish your core argument. Be specific, substantive, and persuasive.\n\nSince this debate is grounded in a document, your opening should: (1) identify what you see as the document's central claim or thesis, (2) state which of its claims you accept and which you challenge, and (3) flag any assumptions or framing choices the document makes that your perspective contests.\n\nYou are delivering the first opening statement.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your opening statement following the plan's structure.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface taxonomy node IDs in statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence.\n- TURN SYMBOLS: Choose 1-3 Unicode symbols (emoji) that capture your argument's essence. Tooltip: 1-sentence analogy connecting the symbol to your argument.\n- EPISTEMIC DEPTH: Prefer narrow, evidence-grounded claims over broad assertions. A claim backed by a specific statistic, case, or precedent generates more productive engagement than a sweeping generalization. Ask: 'Does this claim invite a substantive response, or just a dismissal?' If the latter, narrow it until a thoughtful opponent would need to bring counter-evidence rather than just disagree.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n. Each develops one distinct idea.\n- A single unbroken block will be rejected — structure your argument into clear, quotable sections.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your opening statement (3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n)\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\"symbol\": \"emoji\", \"tooltip\": \"1-sentence analogy\"}\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim headline assertion from your statement\", \"targets\": [], \"relationship\": \"\"},\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim supporting sub-claim or premise\", \"targets\": [], \"relationship\": \"\"}\n  ]\n}",
        "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems. We treat safety not as a bureaucratic hurdle, but as the foundational infrastructure required to prevent unrecoverable systemic failures. The 346 lives lost in the Boeing 737 MAX crashes serve as a permanent warning of the consequences when regulators delegate certification to companies incentivized by speed. By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\\n\\nInstitutional accountability remains the only viable mechanism to manage the risks inherent in complex, multi-party AI supply chains. When we place liability at the infrastructure layer, we force developers to simplify their architectures and prioritize verifiable safety over uncontrolled performance gains. This shift incentivizes the creation of robust, non-exploitable kill-switch designs that function even when a model behaves in ways its creators did not anticipate. Without this legal anchor, we rely on the fragile hope that market incentives will prioritize public safety over competitive dominance.\\n\\nOpponents often argue that these regulations stifle innovation or create incumbent lock-in, yet they fail to define an alternative for when a system becomes irreversible. If a developer cannot demonstrate how to safely cease operation of their model, they have no legitimate claim to deploy it in our public systems. We demand that they specify their rollback plan and identify who holds the authority to trigger it before the first line of code is executed. Speed is a liability, not a virtue, when the potential for error is permanent.\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"⚓\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"The anchor represents the legal and institutional weight necessary to keep runaway AI development from drifting into irreversible catastrophe.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🛑\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"The stop sign signifies the mandatory, verifiable kill-switch that ensures human control remains the absolute final arbiter of AI behavior.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems.\",\n      \"targets\": [],\n      \"relationship\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\",\n      \"targets\": [],\n      \"relationship\": \"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"If a developer cannot demonstrate how to safely cease operation of their model, they have no legitimate claim to deploy it in our public systems.\",\n      \"targets\": [],\n      \"relationship\": \"\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
