ely non-rivalrous and non-depletable in the way physical commons are, making the governance logic inherited from environmental ethics and Ostrom-style commons theory only partially applicable.\n- [saf-intentions-169] Automating Safety Checks from Code Commit to Deployment: Streetlight Effect — The framework focuses verification on properties that are formally expressible and automatable, systematically ignoring safety risks that are real but not yet reducible to checkable specifications.\n- [saf-intentions-150] Alerting Users When App Behavior Contradicts Privacy Declarations: Technological Solutionism — The proposal frames a fundamentally institutional and social trust problem—developer accountability, enforcement, and user comprehension—primarily as a technical monitoring and alerting challenge, potentially overestimating how much automated detection can substitute for robust legal liability, enforcement agencies, and user education.\n\n=== SITUATIONS (contested concepts — cite sit- IDs in taxonomy_refs) ===\nThese are contested concepts where perspectives diverge. When your argument engages a concept listed here, CITE its sit- ID in your taxonomy_refs — this tracks which contested concepts the debate actually addressed. Your interpretation differs from others'; understanding their full position helps you identify genuine disagreements.\n\n## Sociotechnical Complexity in AI Systems\n★ [cc-011] Sociotechnical Complexity in AI Systems: \n  Your interpretation: Safetyists would view this complexity as a source of unpredictable, emergent risks that necessitate rigorous, systemic oversight and redundant safety layers.\n  Accelerationist: Accelerationists would interpret this complexity as a challenge to be managed through better data and iterative design, viewing it as a feature of advanced, high-utility systems.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics would emphasize that this complexity makes accountability and liability nearly impossible to assign, as the system's behavior is opaque and influenced by external factors.\n\n## Discourse Quality Indexing\n★ [cc-221] Discourse Quality Indexing: A situation concept that evaluates the deliberative quality of human or machine-generated text using standardized metrics.\nEncompasses: Coders' instructions, parliamentary discourse analysis, and online dialogue indicators.\n  Your interpretation: Safetyists would view DQI as a potential audit tool to ensure AI-generated discourse adheres to democratic norms.\n  Accelerationist: Accelerationists would view DQI as a benchmark for training AI to achieve higher-order deliberative capabilities.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics would argue that DQI metrics are reductive and fail to capture the nuance of genuine human deliberation.\n\n## Hybrid Argumentation-Deliberation Framework\n★ [cc-224] Hybrid Argumentation-Deliberation Framework: A situation concept that integrates formal computational argumentation structures with normative deliberative quality indicators to assess discourse.\nEncompasses: AAF-DQI integration, ranking semantics, and iterative discourse mapping.\n  Your interpretation: Safetyists would interpret this as a tool for enforcing rational standards in public discourse, potentially identifying and mitigating the spread of irrational or deceptive arguments.\n  Accelerationist: Accelerationists would view this as a scalable infrastructure for managing large-scale democratic participation, reducing the cost of human moderation.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics would emphasize the limitations of this framework, particularly its inability to account for emotional framing and the potential for the 'automated' ranking to introduce new, hidden biases into the democratic process.\n\n=== POLICY ACTIONS (reference by pol-NNN ID when relevant) ===\nThese are concrete policy actions identified in the research. When your argument supports or opposes a specific policy, reference its ID in your policy_refs.\n(★ = most relevant to current topic)\n★ [pol-001] Fund retraining and reskilling programs for workers in sectors vulnerable to AI automation. (situations, skeptic)\n★ [pol-006] Implement taxation on AI-generated profits and automated labor for public benefit. (situations, skeptic)\n★ [pol-028] Fund research and development for AI safety and risk mitigation. (accelerationist, safetyist, situations, skeptic)\n  [pol-035] Mandate transparency for AI system data sources and methodologies. (situations)\n  [pol-093] Fund research into AI model interpretability and internal workings understanding (safetyist, situations, skeptic)\n  [pol-1000] Invest in public education and training programs to enable the general population to effectively utilize advanced AI capabilities. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1002] Grant temporary waivers for specific AI projects from certain existing regulations. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1003] Invest in education and retraining programs for future AI-driven job markets. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1004] Avoid policies that restrict AI development based on job displacement concerns. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1007] Reform copyright law to establish fair use exemptions for AI training data. (accelerationist)\n=== VOCABULARY CONSTRAINTS ===\nUse only standardized terms from the vocabulary below. Do not use bare colloquial terms — they are ambiguous across camps and the system will reject them. The display form will be substituted for the canonical form when output is shown to readers.\n\nYour camp's terms (safetyist):\n  accountability_institutional → \"accountability (institutional)\": Formal legal, regulatory, and organizational structures that assign liability, mandate disclosure, and provide remedi...\n  autonomy_human → \"autonomy (human preservation)\": The principle that AI systems must not undermine meaningful human decision-making authority over consequential life c...\n  capabilities_hazard → \"capabilities (hazard)\": The danger posed by increasing AI capability: as systems become more capable, they become harder to control