d implement verifiable, non-exploitable kill-switch architectures.\",\n      \"taxonomy_anchor\": \"saf-intentions-160\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"framing_choices\": [\n    {\n      \"frame\": \"Institutional Stewardship\",\n      \"why\": \"It shifts the burden of proof onto those advocating for rapid deployment, framing them as reckless actors who are gambling with established civilizational safeguards.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"frame\": \"Irreversibility as the Regulatory Trigger\",\n      \"why\": \"It forces opponents to address the 'rollback' problem—if they cannot demonstrate how to halt a system once it exceeds human control, they have no valid argument for deployment.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"anticipated_challenges\": [\n    \"Opponents will argue that regulation stifles competitiveness and creates incumbent lock-in.\",\n    \"Opponents will challenge the technical feasibility of universal 'kill-switches' at the cloud level.\",\n    \"I am exposing the assumption that 'speed' is a proxy for progress, and the assumption that market forces alone can manage existential, non-reversible risks.\"\n  ]\n}\n\n=== DRAFT STATEMENT ===\n{\n  \"statement\": \"H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems. We treat safety not as a bureaucratic hurdle, but as the foundational infrastructure required to prevent unrecoverable systemic failures. The 346 lives lost in the Boeing 737 MAX crashes serve as a permanent warning of the consequences when regulators delegate certification to companies incentivized by speed. By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\\n\\nInstitutional accountability remains the only viable mechanism to manage the risks inherent in complex, multi-party AI supply chains. When we place liability at the