eability versus narrow/technical definitions, ignoring intermediate standards (e.g., sector-specific, tiered, or probabilistic foreseeability frameworks) that might balance adaptability with predictability.\n- [acc-intentions-103] Prohibit Vendors from Imposing Safety Restrictions Beyond the Law on Government AI: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as either full government access for any lawful use or crippling private vetoes, ignoring middle-ground frameworks such as negotiated use-case carve-outs, independent oversight boards, or tiered access agreements.\n- [acc-intentions-103] Prohibit Vendors from Imposing Safety Restrictions Beyond the Law on Government AI: Appeal to Legality — The argument treats 'lawful use' as a sufficient ethical and safety justification for AI deployment, conflating legal permissibility with moral acceptability — a non sequitur given how far law often lags behind technology.\n- [acc-intentions-099] Integrating Mandatory Pre-Deployment Auditing with Strict Liability Enforcement: false dilemma — The node frames the choice as binary—either pre-deployment safety gates or post-deployment monitoring with strict liability—when in practice most robust governance regimes combine both, and treating them as mutually exclusive is a structural misrepresentation.\n- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as binary — entirely new frameworks versus unchanged old ones — ignoring that existing legal doctrines (tort, contract, administrative law) can be substantially reinterpreted or supplemented without wholesale replacement.\n\n=== SITUATIONS (contested concepts — cite sit- IDs in taxonomy_refs) ===\nThese are contested concepts where perspectives diverge. When your argument engages a concept listed here, CITE its sit- ID in your taxonomy_refs — this tracks which contested concepts the debate actually addressed. Your interpretation differs from others'; understanding their full position helps you identify genuine disagreements.\n\n## The Regulatory Obsolescence Trap\n★ [cc-107] The Regulatory Obsolescence Trap: A situation concept that describes the inherent failure of static, slow-moving legal frameworks to govern technologies that evolve at exponential, non-linear speeds. \nEncompasses: Regulatory lag, rapid technological advancement, policy obsolescence.\n  Your interpretation: Accelerationists would argue this proves that regulation is fundamentally incapable of keeping up and should therefore be abandoned in favor of market-led innovation.\n  Safetyist: Safetyists would use this to argue that because regulation is too slow, we must prioritize intrinsic safety norms and international cooperation to manage risks before they manifest.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics would interpret this as evidence that we should focus on immediate, manageable harms rather than attempting to regulate future, unpredictable capabilities.\n\n## Many Eyes Theory\n★ [cc-092] Many Eyes Theory: A Belief within accelerationist and safetyist discourse that public scrutiny of system design leads to more effective bug detection and mitigation.\nEncompasses: Linus's Law, bug bounty programs, and open-source security auditing.\n  Your interpretation: Accelerationists interpret this as a market-driven mechanism for quality control, where open access to system details allows for rapid, decentralized improvement of AI capabilities.\n  Safetyist: Safetyists view this as a necessary condition for independent auditing and red-teaming, ensuring that vulnerabilities are identified by a diverse set of actors before they can be exploited.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics view this as a necessary but insufficient condition, noting that 'Many Eyes' requires institutional support and protection for researchers to be effective.\n\n## Regulatory-Marketplace Mismatch\n★ [cc-140] Regulatory-Marketplace Mismatch: A situation concept that describes the friction between the need for