e\", \"targets\": [], \"relationship\": \"\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nNOTE: At least one claim_sketch MUST have a non-empty \"targets\" array referencing a prior speaker's AN node, with a support/neutral relationship. Allowed opening relationships: \"extends\", \"integrates\", \"concedes_and_pivots\", \"specifies\".",
            "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"H.R. 9333 attempts to govern the future of intelligence with the machinery of a 1970s utility commission, effectively mandating that developers build a kill-switch into the very fabric of recursive, distributed systems. Safetyist argues that this is a necessary hedge against unrecoverable systemic risk, while Skeptic correctly identifies that such mandates invite massive, unquantified compliance costs. Both are right that we face a coordination problem, but they are wrong to assume that centralized, top-down control is the remedy for a decentralized, non-linear technology. The bill assumes that safety is a static state achieved by gatekeeping, when in reality, security in the AI era is an emergent property of open, transparent, and rapidly iterating architectures.\\n\\nMandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development in favor of closed, incumbent-controlled silos. Historical precedents from the software era, such as the initial regulatory friction following the 1996 Telecommunications Act, demonstrate that when liability is placed on the infrastructure layer, innovation migrates to foreign, less-regulated jurisdictions within 18 to 24 months. By imposing these costs on cloud providers, H.R. 9333 ensures that only the largest, most entrenched firms can afford the legal overhead of deployment. This is not a safety framework; it is a structural mandate for institutional stagnation that sacrifices our competitive edge for the illusion of domestic control.\\n\\nOperational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control that ignores how modern inference actually functions. Because these models are increasingly deployed across local edge devices and decentralized clusters, a centralized off-switch is a performative gesture that fails to address the reality of open-weight proliferation. 