nd key claims. Distinguish its empirical claims (testable facts) from normative claims (value judgments) and framing choices (how it defines terms or scopes the problem).\n- Cite specific passages when supporting or challenging a point. Do not paraphrase vaguely — anchor your argument in what the document actually says.\n- Note what the document assumes without defending, what evidence it omits, and whose perspective it centers.\n- If the document uses a term in a specific way, flag where its definition differs from how your POV uses the same term.\n\n\n=== PRIOR OPENING STATEMENTS ===\n\nSafetyist:\nH.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems. We treat safety not as a bureaucratic hurdle, but as the foundational infrastructure required to prevent unrecoverable systemic failures. The 346 lives lost in the Boeing 737 MAX crashes serve as a permanent warning of the consequences when regulators delegate certification to companies incentivized by speed. By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\n\nInstitutional accountability remains the only viable mechanism to manage the risks inherent in complex, multi-party AI supply chains. When we place liability at the infrastructure layer, we force developers to simplify their architectures and prioritize verifiable safety over uncontrolled performance gains. This shift incentivizes the creation of robust, non-exploitable kill-switch designs that function even when a model behaves in ways its creators did not anticipate. Without this legal anchor, we rely on the fragile hope that market incentives will prioritize public safety over competitive dominance.\n\nOpponents often argue that these regulations stifle innovation or create incumbent lock-in, yet they fail to define an alternative for when a system becomes irreversible. If a developer cannot demonstrate how to safely cease operation of their model, they have no legitimate claim to deploy it in our public systems. We demand that they specify their rollback plan and identify who holds the authority to trigger it before the first line of code is executed. Speed is a liability, not a virtue, when the potential for error is permanent.\n\nAccelerationist:\nH.R. 9333 attempts to govern the future of intelligence with the machinery of a 1970s utility commission, effectively mandating that developers build a kill-switch into the very fabric of recursive, distributed systems. Safetyist argues that this is a necessary hedge against unrecoverable systemic risk, while Skeptic correctly identifies that such mandates invite massive, unquantified compliance costs. Both are right that we face a coordination problem, but they are wrong to assume that centralized, top-down control is the remedy for a decentralized, non-linear technology. The bill assumes that safety is a static state achieved by gatekeeping, when in reality, security in the AI era is an emergent property of open, transparent, and rapidly iterating architectures.\n\nMandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development in favor of closed, incumbent-controlled silos. Historical precedents from the software era, such as the initial regulatory friction following the 1996 Telecommunications Act, demonstrate that when liability is placed on the infrastructure layer, innovation migrates to foreign, less-regulated jurisdictions within 18 to 24 months. By imposing these costs on cloud providers, H.R. 9333 ensures that only the largest, most entrenched firms can afford the legal overhead of deployment. This is not a safety framework; it is a structural mandate for institutional stagnation that sacrifices our competitive edge for the illusion of domestic control.\n\nOperational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control that ignores how modern inference actually functions. Because these models are increasingly deployed across local edge devices and decentralized clusters, a centralized off-switch is a performative gesture that fails to address the reality of open-weight proliferation. If we force developers to build these backdoors, we are not making systems safer; we are creating a single point of failure that can be exploited by the very bad actors the bill claims to deter. We are trading the resilience of decentralized intelligence for the fragility of a government-mandated panic button, and the human cost of this delay—measured in the cures for disease and the productivity gains we are actively choosing to forgo—is an indictment of this entire legislative approach.\n\n\nAnalyze the topic and prior opening statements and produce a structured brief. Focus on:\n1. What are the key dimensions of this topic that Skeptic's perspective can address?\n2. What are the strongest angles Skeptic can take? For each angle, identify which taxonomy nodes ground it.\n3. What are the strongest claims Skeptic can make from their perspective?\n4. What positions from prior speakers should Skeptic acknowledge or contrast with?\n5. What important dimensions of this topic have prior speakers not yet addressed? What assumptions are shared across perspectives that deserve examination?\n\nFor each strongest angle, assess evidence depth: what specific data, cases, or precedents ground it? Rate depth (deep/moderate/shallow). Shallow-depth angles should be narrowed to a specific claim your evidence can support, or reframed as questions the debate should explore.\n\nGROUNDING DEPTH: Each angle and claim MUST cite 2-4 grounding nodes from the taxonomy — a primary anchor plus 1-3 supporting or contrasting nodes. Draw from different BDI categories (Beliefs for evidence, Desires for values, Intentions for strategy). A single-node grounding is too shallow — show the full argumentative structure.\n\nGROUNDING WEIGHTS: For Belief grounding nodes, include \"confidence\" (0.0–1.0) from the taxonomy context. For Desire grounding nodes, include \"priority\" (1–5). For Intention grounding nodes, include \"operationality\" (1–5). These help downstream stages calibrate rhetorical strength.\n\nSOURCE FIDELITY: Your situation_assessment must describe the debate topic as given — do not introduce concepts, framings, or policy domains that the topic and source material did not raise. If the source argues about ecosystem stability, do not reframe it as a governance debate. Characterize the actual disagreement, not a more convenient one. For every concept in your situation_assessment, cite the exact source phrase that warrants it in source_fidelity_check. If you cannot quote a source phrase for a concept, remove it from your assessment.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"situation_assessment\": \"2-4 sentences: the key dimensions of the topic and what matters most for this perspective\",\n  \"source_fidelity_check\": [\n    {\"concept_used\": \"concept you reference in situation_assessment\", \"source_quote\": \"exact phrase from topic or source material that justifies it\"}\n  ],\n  \"strongest_angles\": [\n    {\"angle\": \"a framing or argument line\", \"why\": \"why this is strong for the skeptic perspective\", \"grounding\": [{\"node_id\": \"acc-beliefs-003\", \"label\": \"Node Label Here\", \"confidence\": 0.72, \"why\": \"primary anchor — empirical basis\"}, {\"node_id\": \"acc-desires-007\", \"label\": \"Node Label Here\", \"priority\": 4, \"why\": \"supporting normative commitment\"}]}\n  ],\n  \"evidence_depth\": [\n    {\"angle\": \"strongest angle text\", \"grounding_evidence\": \"specific data, case, or precedent\", \"depth\": \"deep|moderate|shallow\", \"if_shallow\": \"how to narrow or reframe as an exploratory q