y\": \"Skeptic can highlight that the bill's focus on cloud providers ignores the reality of decentralized, local-run AI.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"prior_positions_to_address\": [\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Safetyist\",\n      \"position\": \"Mandating cloud-provider liability and kill-switches is necessary for civilizational stability.\",\n      \"response_strategy\": \"Challenge the assumption that centralized liability prevents systemic failure; argue it instead creates a single point of failure and regulatory capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Accelerationist\",\n      \"position\": \"The bill is a 1970s-style regulatory failure that will force innovation into foreign jurisdictions.\",\n      \"response_strategy\": \"Acknowledge the critique of regulatory obsolescence, but contrast by focusing on the specific harm of incumbent entrenchment rather than just 'innovation velocity'.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nPlan your opening statement strategy. This is your first appearance — you need to:\n1. Establish your core position clearly and memorably\n2. Choose which 2-4 taxonomy nodes to build your argument around\n3. Decide on the argumentative structure (claim + evidence + warrant for each main point)\n4. Identify 1-2 specific claims from prior speakers to build on — name the claim and whether you EXTEND it (add supporting evidence), INTEGRATE it with your perspective, or use CONCEDE AND PIVOT (acknowledge its strength, then show where your evidence diverges). Strong openings advance the conversation, not just add to it. Rank your own claims by evidence depth — lead with your most grounded claim.\n\n=== FIELD-AWARE STRATEGY ===\nYour taxonomy nodes have epistemic_type, rhetorical_strategy, falsifiability, and assumes fields. Use them in planning:\n\nEPISTEMIC TYPE — match argument mode to claim type:\n- empirical_claim → argue with evidence\n- normative_prescription → argue from coherence/values\n- strategic_recommendation → challenge feasibility, cite analogous case