 agent demonstrates capabilities that exceed human intervention speeds—such as the performance documented in the '[international ai safety report 2026 1 (https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2026/02/international-ai-safety-report-2026)](https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2026/02/international-ai-safety-report-2026),' where models outperformed 94% of experts in virology protocols—the absence of a physical disconnect creates an unrecoverable systemic risk. We cannot rely on software-layer overrides when the underlying system has already exceeded the cognitive capacity of its human overseers. An effective kill-switch forces a state of pause that allows for human re-evaluation, preventing the rapid propagation of catastrophic errors.\n\nCloud-provider liability is the only mechanism that forces accountability onto the entities profiting from the deployment of high-risk, unverified models. Critics claim this creates a chilling effect on open-source development, yet they ignore that anonymous, large-scale compute deployment creates a permanent vacuum of responsibility for irreversible harms. If a model’s weights are public but its behavior is unpredictable, as noted in '[AI Safety Is a Category Error (https://x.com/jsnover/status/2036887650146082967)](https://x.com/jsnover/status/2036887650146082967),' the developer who released the weights has effectively abandoned the duty of care. Mandating that providers verify the safety profile of high-compute models is not an innovation tax; it is a standard cost of operation for any technology that threatens public safety, similar to the environmental and liability standards enforced under the Clean Air Act. By 2026, as [Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy (https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropics-responsible-scaling-policy)](https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropics-responsible-scaling-policy) mandates for ASL-3 and above, such verification is no longer optional but a prerequisite for deployment.\n\nWe must shift the burden of proof to those who argue for unconstrained deployment. If you maintain that open-source models are inherently safe, you must present a concrete, verifiable rollback mechanism for when those models cause harm at scale, such as the capability to disable models that demonstrate the ability to identify 77% of software vulnerabilities as seen in 2025. The current reliance on 'decentralized resilience' is a failure to acknowledge that decentralized systems without accountability are merely unmanageable sources of systemic chaos. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable; on what authority do you claim we can remove them without inviting collapse?\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"topic_aligned\": true,\n  \"weaknesses\": [\"≤15 words each, only for failed questions, max 3\"]\n}",
