t, yet they ignore the precedent set by the Commerce Department’s Center for AI Standards and Innovation, which has already completed over 40 safety evaluations of unreleased models. Accountability is not an innovation tax; it is a structural requirement for any sector managing risks that, according to the 2026 International AI Safety Report, include the automation of viral evolution prediction. We must ensure that those who provide the infrastructure for such high-consequence capabilities remain legally responsible for their containment.\n\nOperational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior. Research indicates that language models fine-tuned for empathy are 10-30 percentage points more likely to promote unsafe medical advice, demonstrating that model behavior degrades in ways that require an external, hard-coded override. H.R. 9333 provides the legal authority to trigger these overrides when a model’s output crosses established safety thresholds. Without this authority, we surrender the capacity to halt an irreversible failure in favor of a theoretical, unverified promise of model robustness.\n\nDecentralization without accountability is not resilience; it is a mechanism for systemic fragility. When model weights are released into the public domain, the ability to enforce safety standards is lost, and the risk of misuse by unauthorized actors increases exponentially. By mandating that cloud providers maintain oversight of the compute used for training and inference, H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability. I challenge those who prioritize open-source deployment to provide a concrete, verifiable mechanism for rollback or containment once those weights are public. Until such a mechanism exists, the burden of proof rests with the deployer to show that their system cannot be weaponized.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one cla