rently Better Understood Through Their Outputs Than Their Internals (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-055] Domain-Specific AI Beats Broadly Competent AI in Practice (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-059] Markets Are Intelligent Systems That Optimize Resource Allocation (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-051] Build an Evolving Catalog of AI Failure Modes (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-019] Foundation Model Capabilities Can Be Systematically Unlocked and Deployed (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-052] Remove Barriers and Increase Funding to Accelerate AI Progress (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-114] Accelerate AI Through Open, Decentralized, and Unrestricted Experimentation (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-054] Ground AI Safety in Observable Behavior and Real-World Testing (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-055] Integrate AI Into Human Work as a Capability Enhancer, Not a Replacement (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-056] Discredit Precautionary AI Regulation as Progress-Blocking (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-059] Shift Organizations from Periodic Reviews to AI-Powered Continuous Steering (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-060] Frame Capitalism and Complex Systems as Information-Processing Entities (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-061] Argue That Scaling General Models Outperforms Building Specialized AI (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-020] AI Feedback Dramatically Accelerates Human Learning (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-desires-018] Build Crisis-Proof Institutions with AI (Desires) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-061] Corporate AI Strategy Follows Consumer Technology Adoption Patterns (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-060] Markets and Technology Form a Self-Reinforcing Growth Engine (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-051] Stagnation, Not Recklessness, Is What Kills Civilizations (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-desires-020] Mandatory AI Education for All (Desires) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-021] AI Models Encode Useful Knowledge That Exists Outside Human Language (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-068] Replace Legacy IT with Adaptive AI Infrastructure (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-desires-021] National Self-Sufficiency in AI Infrastructure (Desires) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-023] Training AI Costs About as Much Energy as Raising a Human (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-024] AGI Is Not a Better Tool — It Is a Fundamentally Superior Intelligence (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-069] Accelerate Prosperity Through AI-Driven Abundance (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-070] Shift AI Competition from Software to Physical Infrastructure Control (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-025] Computational Power Is the New Measure of National Strength (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-026] Civilization's Future Is Digital, Not Biological (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-071] Secure Early Access to Human-Generated Training Data as a Strategic Asset (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-027] The Weakest Link in AI Development Is the Human Developer (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-072] Use Self-Fulfilling Narratives to Accelerate Technological Outcomes (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-028] AI Audit Requirements Disproportionately Burden Small Labs Unless Publicly Funded (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-073] Governing AI Risk Through Market-Based Insurance and Post-Deployment Liability (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-074] Federalize AI Regulation to Prevent State-by-State Fragmentation (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-075] Automate Permits, Licenses, and Compliance Checks with AI Agents (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-beliefs-029] Startups Drive Innovation More Than Big Tech (Beliefs) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-076] Prioritize Energy, Permitting, and Data Centers Over Software Regulation (Intentions) — accelerationist\n[acc-desires-023] Genetic Selection as Parental Responsibility (Desires) — accelerationist\n[acc-intentions-078] Create Regulatory Conditions Where Market Competition Drives AI Transparency (Intentions) — accelerationist\n\nARGUMENTS RAISED SO FAR:\n  1. H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems.\n  2. By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\n  3. H.R. 9333 is a structural mandate for institutional stagnation that sacrifices our competitive edge for the illusion of domestic control.\n  4. Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.\n  5. Operational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control.\n  6. Fact-check (disputed): The claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.\n  7. H.R. 9333 is a regulatory moat that entrenches incumbents by offloading liability onto cloud providers.\n  8. The mandate for kill-switches is a technical fantasy that fails to account for the proliferation of local, edge-deployed AI models.\n  9. H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider liability to address the accountability gap inherent in deploying systems that can already outperform 94% of human experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.\n  10. Operational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior.\n  11. The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.\n  12. By embedding safety checks at the silicon level, we move from reactive, software-only patches to a proactive, verifiable containment layer.\n\nYOUR TASK: Identify 1-2 strong arguments that NONE of the debaters have made and that their assigned perspectives would be unlikely to make. Focus on:\n- Cross-cutting positions that synthesize elements from multiple perspectives\n- Compromise proposals that no single perspective would champion\n- Blind spots where all three perspectives share an unstated assumption\n- Strong arguments that are \"homeless\" — too nuanced for any single camp\n\nFor each argument:\n- State it in 1-2 sentences as a clear, specific claim (not vague platitudes)\n- Explain WHY no debater would make it given their assigned worldview\n- Classify the gap type: cross_cutting, compromise, blind_spot, or unstated_assumption\n- Identify which perspectives SHOULD engage with it (even if they wouldn't originate it)\n- Classify the BDI layer: belief (empirical claim), desire (normative commitment), or intention (strategic reasoning)\n\nRespond with JSON:\n{\n  \"gap_arguments\": [\n    {\n      \"argument\": \"...\",\n      \"why_missing\": \"...\",\n      \"gap_type\": \"cross_cutting | compromise | blind_spot | unstated_assumption\",\n      \"relevant_povs\": [\"accelerationist\", \"safetyist\", \"skeptic\"],\n      \"bdi_layer\": \"belief | desire | intention\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
