tation creates a centralized 'master key' vulnerability that is more dangerous than the software risks they claim to solve, while exposing the regulatory capture inherent in H.R. 9333.\",\n  \"planned_moves\": [\n    {\n      \"move\": \"UNDERCUT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-11\",\n      \"detail\": \"Expose the 'single point of failure' inherent in hardware-level attestation by highlighting the history of state-mandated backdoors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-14\",\n      \"detail\": \"Acknowledge the reality of global supply chain fragmentation, then pivot to how H.R. 9333 uses this to force a 'regulatory moat' for domestic incumbents.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"BURDEN-SHIFT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-11\",\n      \"detail\": \"Demand the Safetyist provide a threat model for when the Department of Commerce's attestation infrastructure is inevitably compromised.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"target_claims\": [\n    \"AN-11\",\n    \"AN-14\"\n  ],\n  \"argument_sketch\": \"Start by stripping away the 'security' label from hardware attestation to show it for what it is: a state-sanctioned master key. Pivot to the material reality of the supply chain to show why this won't stop bad actors but will effectively kill open-source competition. Close by asking who is held liable when the government's own attestation server is breached.\",\n  \"anticipated_responses\": [\n    \"Safetyist will argue that centralized control is a necessary trade-off for national security.\",\n    \"Accelerationist will likely agree on the 'moat' argument but ignore the inherent risks of their own decentralized models.\"\n  ],\n  \"target_nodes\": [\n    \"skp-beliefs-235\",\n    \"skp-beliefs-249\",\n    \"skp-desires-005\",\n    \"skp-intentions-141\"\n  ]\n}\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- skp-beliefs-235\n- skp-beliefs-249\n- skp-desires-005\n- skp-intentions-141\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxonomy_refs.\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nAddress Safetyist on this point: Address the feasibility of hardware-level attestation (AN-11) raised by Safetyist, specifically responding to the critique of supply chain fragmentation and the risk of state-mandated backdoors (AN-14).\n\nProbe deeper. Find cruxes, test edge cases, and name areas of agreement explicitly.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your debate statement following the plan's structure and moves. Stay in character as Skeptic.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n. Each paragraph develops one distinct idea.\n- A single unbroken block will be rejected — structure your argument into clear, quotable sections.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- ATTRIBUTION FIDELITY: You may only attribute positions to other debaters that they have explicitly stated in the debate history. Do not infer, extrapolate, or fabricate positions. Phrases like \"your solution is X\" or \"you're arguing for Y\" must correspond to something actually said — not an implication you've constructed. Misrepresenting another debater's position undermines the debate's integrity and will be flagged.\n- TOPIC PREMISE FIDELITY: Structural features stated in the debate topic are givens, not claims. Critique their consequences, not their existence.\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface AN-IDs or taxonomy node IDs in your statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence and note which prior claims it engages with.\n- TURN SYMBOLS: Choose 1-3 Unicode symbols (emoji) that capture your argument's essence. Tooltip: 1-sentence analogy connecting the symbol to your argument.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"OpenAI transitioned from a nonprofit entity to a 'capped profit' structure with significant investment from Microsoft.\"\n      — \"Constructing AI Speech\" (2015-2023) → OpenAI Corporate Structure Evolution\n  [2] \"Both the American public and AI experts surveyed express a desire for more control over artificial intelligence technology in their lives.\"\n      — \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (Aug-Oct 2024) → Desire for AI Control\n  [3] \"Seven established AI companies, including Google and Meta, account for more than 35% of the entire S&P 500 market capitalization.\"\n      — \"Transparency in AI is on the decline\" (2026-04) → AI Industry Market Concentration\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The document argues that AI should be regulated to prevent monopolistic control and ensure benefits are shared. It warns against a 'tech royalty' scenario where a few companies control essential services. This aligns with the skeptic goal of preventi\n      Quote: \"If premium users get AI that works and the rest of us get a slippery, halfway-functioning version, society becomes even more unequal.\"\n      — \"treat-ai-like-public-utility-2026-2\" (skeptic, aligned)\n  [5] The document explicitly points to the recent U.S. turn toward antitrust enforcement against Big Tech as a viable path for regulating AI. This supports the skeptic desire to dismantle concentrated algorithmic power by treating AI systems as products o\n      Quote: \"The recent U.S. (re)turn to antitrust enforcement against Big Tech illustrates this potential move.\"\n      — \"Constructing AI Speech\" (skeptic, aligned)\n\nYou MUST cite at least one source from the evidence above by title in your statement.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"Constructing AI Speech\" — OpenAI transitioned from a nonprofit entity to a 'capped profit' structure with significant investment from Microsoft.\n2. \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (2025) — A significant minority of both the general public (30%) and AI experts (24%) believe it is extremely or very likely that AI will make humans happier in the next 20 years.\n3. \"Transparency in AI is on the decline\" — Seven established AI companies, including Google and Meta, account for more than 35% of the entire S&P 500 market capitalization.\n4. \"Extreme power concentration   80,000 Hours\" — At its peak, the Manhattan project employed 130,000 people.\n5. \"Extreme power concentration   80,000 Hours\" — The Khmer Rouge regime murdered a quarter of all Cambodians between 1975 and 1979.\n6. \"SUPREMACY  The Psychological Rivalry and Corporate Capture of the Race for Artificial General Intelligence   by shebbar   Medium\" — Microsoft invested $1 billion in OpenAI in exchange for priority access to its AI technology.\n7. \"Computing Power and the Governance of Artificial Intelligence\" — Building new semiconductor fabrication capacity requires multi-year timelines and investments totaling billions of dollars.\n8. \"World Development Report 2026: Artificial Intelligence for Development\" (2026) — As of April 2024, 56 percent of open-source data sets on Hugging Face were in English.\n9. \"Transparency in AI is on the decline\" — Ten major AI companies, including Google, OpenAI, and Anthropic, disclose no information regarding their environmental impact, such as energy usage, carbon emissions, or water use.\n10. \"The Regulatory Tide Goes Out: What Global AI Governance Retrenchment Means for Organizations\" — The EU AI Act's high-risk AI system requirements were originally set to apply beginning August 2, 2026.\n11. \"Is AI sovereignty possible  Balancing autonomy and interdependence   Brookings\" — India is hosting the February 2026 AI Impact Summit as a leader in AI sovereignty initiatives.\n12. \"AI and Democracy: Mapping the Intersections\" — A 2025 Brookings study found no notable change in the proportion of workers in occupations exposed to AI since the launch of ChatGPT in 2022.\n13. \"The State of Sovereign AI: Exploring the Role of Open Source Projects and Global Collaboration in Global AI Strategy\" — 93% of survey respondents agree that open collaboration is essential for building secure and culturally aligned sovereign AI systems.\n14. \"Harvard Law Professor Explains the AI Battle Between Tech and Government\" — Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act of 1996 provides that online platforms are generally not liable for unlawful content posted by third parties.\n15. \"Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence\" — Full-stack AI sovereignty is structurally infeasible for almost any country due to the transnational nature of the AI value chain.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your full debate response (3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n)\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\"symbol\": \"emoji\", \"tooltip\": \"1-sentence analogy\"}\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim sentence from your statement\", \"targets\": [\"AN-3\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim supporting sub-claim\", \"targets\": []}\n  ]\n}",
        "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"Hardware-level attestation is not a safety feature; it is a state-sanctioned master key that guarantees a single point of failure. When the Department of Commerce mandates that silicon manufacturers bake backdoors into every chip, they aren't protecting the public from rogue algorithms. They are building a surveillance architecture that can be breached by any state actor or sophisticated criminal syndicate with enough leverage to compromise a single, centralized attestation server. We don't need to speculate about this risk. We know from historical precedents that once a 'security' backdoor is created, it is only a matter of time before it is weaponized by the very people it was intended to keep out.\\n\\nThis legislative framework effectively constructs a regulatory moat that protects domestic incumbents while crushing open-source competition. As noted in 'Transparency in AI is on the decline,' seven companies already account for over 35% of the S&P 500 market cap, and H.R. 9333 will only accelerate this consolidation. By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model where only the biggest firms can afford the cost of legal operation. This isn't about safety; it is about ensuring that the compute-heavy future remains the private property of a handful of corporations that, according to the same report, currently refuse to disclose their basic water and energy usage metrics.\\n\\nLet’s drop the pretense that we can secure a globalized, fragmented supply chain through top-down mandates. Semiconductors move through dozens of countries and hundreds of specialized foundries before they ever hit a server rack. Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation. When you force this architecture, you aren't creating a kill-switch for 'high-consequence AI'; you are creating a massive, brittle bottleneck that will inevitably break. If the Department of Commerce manages to force this through, who exactly is held liable when the inevitable breach of their attestation infrastructure exposes the entire national grid to control by an adversary?\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🔑\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"Hardware-level attestation functions as a universal master key that creates a catastrophic single point of failure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🏰\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"The regulatory framework acts as a moat, protecting incumbent tech giants from competition while offloading liability.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Hardware-level attestation is not a safety feature; it is a state-sanctioned master key that guarantees a single point of failure.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-11\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-14\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores the reality of a globalized, fragmented supply chain.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-14\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
