sic water and energy usage metrics.\n\nLet’s drop the pretense that we can secure a globalized, fragmented supply chain through top-down mandates. Semiconductors move through dozens of countries and hundreds of specialized foundries before they ever hit a server rack. Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in '[Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence (https://muckrack.com/cameron-f-kerry/articles)](https://muckrack.com/cameron-f-kerry/articles),' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation. When you force this architecture, you aren't creating a kill-switch for 'high-consequence AI'; you are creating a massive, brittle bottleneck that will inevitably break. If the Department of Commerce manages to force this through, who exactly is held liable when the inevitable breach of their attestation infrastructure exposes the entire national grid to control by an adversary?\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument acr