chine-readable compliance audits that lower, rather than raise, the barrier to entry for smaller firms. (Why missing: undefined)\n\nMODERATOR INTERVENTION (this round):\nThe moderator has issued a POLICY_CHALLENGE [elicitation] intervention.\n\nModerator's text:\n\"Skeptic must now move beyond abstract opposition to address the specific technical feasibility of hardware-level attestation, as the current debate has stalled on whether decentralized cryptographic verification can exist without creating a centralized 'master key' vulnerability. While Skeptic argues that Section 402 of H.R. 9333 creates a brittle, state-controlled bottleneck, they have yet to engage with the technical counter-arguments in AN-14 regarding whether secure enclaves can function via local, trustless proofs that bypass the need for a central attestation server. 'If the Department of Commerce manages to force this through, who exactly is held liable when the inevitable breach of their attestation infrastructure exposes the entire national grid to control by an adversary?' Skeptic asks, yet this framing assumes that decentralized implementation is impossible. To advance this discussion, Skeptic must clarify: do they oppose hardware-level attestation because they believe all implementations inherently require a centralized backdoor, or do they support a modular, open-source-compatible cryptographic standard that avoids the 'master key' risk they fear? Please provide a concrete alternative to the Section 402 framework that maintains safety baselines without triggering the systemic consolidation Skeptic describes.\"\n\nMANDATORY RESPONSE FORMAT:\nYour first paragraph MUST begin with \"The mechanism I propose is [specific enforcement/regulatory mechanism].\" Follow with: who implements it, under what legal authority, and what the primary obstacle to implementation is. Then a paragraph break before your substantive argument.\n\nBREVITY RULE: The response paragraph must be SHORT — 2-3 sentences maximu