              "statement": "The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc. This system, enforced by the Federal Trade Commission under existing consumer protection and unfair competition statutes, replaces the need for centralized government 'master keys' by focusing on verifiable operational transparency rather than pre-clearance. The primary obstacle to this implementation is the intense lobbying of incumbent firms that benefit from the opaque, proprietary nature of current hardware supply chains.\n\nHardware-level attestation as proposed in Section 402 is a fantasy that ignores the material reality of globalized supply chains. You cannot secure a silicon architecture through a centralized mandate when the design, fabrication, and packaging of chips occur across disparate jurisdictions, from Taiwan to Ireland. As noted in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' full-stack sovereignty is structurally infeasible, meaning any attempt to force a single, state-controlled 'kill-switch' into this web will simply be bypassed or ignored by global actors. This is not a security feature; it is a political chokepoint that prioritizes the centralization of control over the actual mitigation of cascading failures.\n\nInstead of chasing the illusion of a master kill-switch, we must adopt the principles of graceful degradation and circuit breakers described in 'AI Safety Is a Category Error.' By requiring that all hardware devices output cryptographically signed logs of their operational status, we shift the burden to post-hoc audits where failures can be identified and mitigated in real-time. This provides the safety we need without creating the single point of failure that a government-held master key represents. Ten major AI companies currently disclose zero information regarding their energy usage or environmental impact, proving that these firms will resist any transparency that reveals their actual operational footprint; our job is to force that data into the light through standardized, auditable protocols.\n\nTrue regulatory effectiveness comes from making it impossible for companies to hide their systemic fragilities, not from giving them an off-switch they will never use. We have seen this movie before; when we allow incumbents to define the compliance standard, they pull up the drawbridge to protect their 35% stake in the S&P 500. A decentralized audit trail forces these firms to play by the same rules as the open-source community, ensuring that safety is a measurable standard rather than an abstract promise. Who is going to audit the auditors when the Department of Commerce is busy managing the keys to the kingdom?",
