te your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"Nucleic acid synthesis screening is currently not legally mandated, meaning not all providers screen their orders.\"\n      — \"Preliminary Taxonomy of AI-Bio Misuse Mitigations\" (2026) → Synthesis Screening Status\n  [2] \"AI tools are frequently used by companies to intensify work, increase employee workloads, and create new forms of stress and burnout.\"\n      — \"AI Doesnâ€™t Reduce Workâ€”It Intensifies It\" (2026-02-09) → AI Intensifies Work and Causes Burnout\n  [3] \"In November 2025, the GTG-1002 cyberattack was documented as the first state-sponsored attack where an AI agent executed 80-90% of operational tasks independently.\"\n      — \"adversarialaithreatmodelingframework-aatmfv3-kaiaizen-2026-1\" → GTG-1002 AI Cyberattack\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The document defines 'Loss of control scenario' as a situation where general-purpose AI systems operate outside of human control with no clear path to recovery. This reflects the safetyist focus on existential risk and the potential for autonomous sy\n      Quote: \"Loss of control scenario: A scenario in which one or more general-purpose AI systems come to operate outside of anyone’s control, with no clear path to regaining control.\"\n      — \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (safetyist, aligned)\n  [5] The document strongly aligns with the safetyist desire to prevent AI's systemic harms by meticulously detailing how race-adjusted clinical algorithms perpetuate and amplify health inequities. It provides numerous examples across cardiology, nephrolog\n      Quote: \"Given their potential to perpetuate or even amplify race-based health inequities, they merit thorough scrutiny.\"\n      — \"NEJMms2004740\" (safetyist, aligned)\n\nYou MUST cite at least one source from the evidence above by title in your statement.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"Preliminary Taxonomy of AI-Bio Misuse Mitigations\" — Frontier AI models have reached 'enabling capability thresholds' that could potentially enable extreme harms if deployed without safeguards.\n2. \"AI Doesnâ€™t Reduce Workâ€”It Intensifies It\" (2026) — AI tools are frequently used by companies to intensify work, increase employee workloads, and create new forms of stress and burnout.\n3. \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (2026) — AI models are increasingly capable of performing complex biological tasks, including protein design and viral evolution prediction, which can be used for both beneficial research and malicious weapon development.\n4. \"NEJMms2004740\" — A 2019 study found that race may influence decisions in heart-failure management, with black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department being less likely than white patients to be admitted to the cardiology service.\n5. \"Google, Microsoft and xAI Agree to Share Early AI Models with U.S.\" — The Commerce Department’s Center for AI Standards and Innovation has completed more than 40 evaluations of AI models, including unreleased ones.\n6. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — AI models have outperformed 94% of domain experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.\n7. \"Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback: The Silent Killer of Data Anonymization\" — Current machine unlearning methods are susceptible to benign relearning attacks that reverse the effects of unlearning.\n8. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — Language models fine-tuned to sound warm or empathetic were found to be 10-30 percentage points more likely to make errors, such as promoting conspiracy theories or offering unsafe medical advice, compared to less empathetic models.\n9. \"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy\" — Current LLMs, including Claude, are classified as ASL-2, showing early signs of dangerous capabilities but lacking sufficient reliability for catastrophic misuse.\n10. \"Monitoring Monitorability\" — Follow-up questions can elicit more tokens for monitoring, but their effectiveness depends on whether they are tailored to the monitor's objective.\n11. \"Incentive Aware AI Regulations: A Credal Characterisation\" — A regulation mechanism can achieve a 'perfect market outcome' if and only if the set of non-compliant distributions forms a credal set (a closed, convex set of probability measures).\n12. \"Artificial intelligence research strategy of the United States: critical assessment and policy assessment and recommendations\" (doi:10.3389/fdata.2023.1206139, 2023) — The US spent more than 600 billion dollars in gross domestic spending on R&D in 2019.\n13. \"AI RMF Playbook\" — AI systems in high-stakes settings like transportation and security can cause physical or environmental harm if they fail, necessitating thorough pre-deployment testing and ongoing evaluation.\n14. \"AI RMF Playbook\" — AI systems are dynamic and may perform in unexpected ways once deployed or after deployment, necessitating continuous monitoring.\n15. \"Classified Networks AI Agreements\" — The GenAI.mil platform has deployed hundreds of thousands of agents in five months.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your full debate response (3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n)\",\n  \"turn_symbols\"