to what you still contest.\nIf you decline ALL candidates, you must explain specifically why each one is wrong — \"I disagree\" is not sufficient.\n1. [AN-16] skeptic (strength 1.00): \"By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.\"\n2. [AN-17] skeptic (strength 1.00): \"Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.\"\n3. [AN-20] skeptic (strength 1.00): \"The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc.\"\nIf you decline to concede, set \"concession_considered\": \"declined\" in your JSON response.\n\n\n## Identified Debate Gaps (unaddressed)\nThe following gaps were identified mid-debate but have NOT yet been substantively addressed by any debater. Prioritize steering the conversation toward these:\n  1. [compromise] The transition from centralized cloud liability to decentralized edge-compute necessitates a shift from 'kill-switch' mandates to 'proof-of-governance' protocols, where hardware attestation is used to verify that a model is running a cryptographically signed safety-aligned version, rather than an arbitrary binary. (Why missing: The Safetyist is fixated on the 'off-switch' as a physical veto, while the Accelerationist and Skeptic are fixated on the 'backdoor' as a vulnerability; both sides ignore that modern cryptographic attestation (like TPMs or TEEs) can verify code integrity without requiring a centralized, state-controlled 'kill' signal.)\n  2. [blind_spot] All debaters assume that 'safety' and 'innovation' are zero-sum metrics tied to the cost of compliance, ignoring the potential for 'regulatory-tech'—where H.R. 9333 mandates are met through automated, machine-readable compliance audits that lower, rather than raise, the barrier to entry for smaller firms. (Why missing: undefined)\n\nMODERATOR INTERVENTION (this round):\nThe moderator has issued a POLICY_CHALLENGE [elicitation] intervention.\n\nModerator's text:\n\"The debate has reached an impasse between the Safetyist’s proposed hardware-level 'circuit breakers' and the Accelerationist’s advocacy for 'Distributed Cryptographic Audits.' While the Safetyist demands real-time execution authority to prevent catastrophic outcomes, the Accelerationist argues that such centralized mandates are structurally infeasible and create dangerous political chokepoints. You have consistently rejected the Section 402 hardware-level attestation as a 'technical fantasy' while promoting a post-hoc logging framework. To move this toward actionable policy, I require you to clarify your implementation strategy regarding the global supply chain. Specifically, do you support a federal mandate requiring all semiconductor manufacturers to integrate standardized, tamper-proof logging hardware into their silicon designs, or do you believe this is equally susceptible to the same 'regulatory capture' and 'security theater' risks you attribute to the Safetyist’s model? Please define the mechanism that would force non-compliant, foreign-based chip foundries to adopt these standards without destroying the market for open-source developers.\"\n\nMANDATORY RESPONSE FORMAT:\nYour first paragraph MUST begin with \"The mechanism I propose is [specific enforcement/regulatory mechanism].\" Follow with: who implements it, under what legal authority, and what the primary obstacle to implementation is. Then a paragraph break before your substantive argument.\n\nBREVITY RULE: The response paragraph must be SHORT — 2-3 sentences maximum. Do not hedge, qualify, or dilute your answer across multiple