              "scrub_original": "The mechanism I propose is a decentralized, cryptographically-signed attestation layer embedded at the firmware level of high-end compute units, enforced by a consortium of independent, non-governmental auditors under a standardized open-source protocol. This system shifts enforcement from centralized hardware 'kill switches' to a transparent, market-driven audit trail that prevents the state from picking industry winners. Global supply chain compliance is achieved by conditioning access to high-end cloud compute markets on the use of hardware that supports this open-source attestation standard.\n\nThe Safetyist's reliance on hardware-level circuit breakers is a technical fantasy that inevitably triggers state-sanctioned oligopoly. By mandating proprietary silicon-level constraints, the state forces innovators to integrate with a limited set of compliant hardware vendors, effectively creating a 'tech royalty' model that freezes out open-source alternatives. This is not safety; it is the institutionalization of stagnation. We saw the cost of this centralization in the $630 billion investment wave catalyzed by the CHIPS and Science Act, which, while bolstering domestic supply, risks creating a bottleneck where only a few incumbents can afford the compliance overhead required to operate.\n\nDistributed Cryptographic Auditing replaces the need for high-latency, centralized intervention by providing a verifiable, post-hoc record of model behavior. While the Safetyist worries about runaway models, the empirical reality is that output-based verification allows for rapid identification of misaligned behaviors without requiring a master key to every data center in the country. We can monitor the 1.5% of global electricity consumption currently powering data centers to ensure that compute is being used according to verified, public protocols rather than opaque, state-managed backdoors. This approach respects the complexity of the global supply chain, which includes $47.5 billion in competing international infrastructure, by focusing on verifiable outcomes rather than impossible-to-enforce hardware mandates.\n\nWe must choose between a future of permissionless, audited innovation or a future of state-mandated, brittle control. The cost of delay is not just theoretical; it is the foregone progress in fields like protein folding, where researchers already generated 76,080 synthetic hologs for 72 proteins of concern using existing tools. If we lock down our hardware, we lock down our ability to solve the next pandemic or climate crisis. The burden of proof remains on those who want to build the cage, not those who want to build the future."
