g\": [\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"sit-032\",\n          \"label\": \"Sovereign AI as Strategic Independence\",\n          \"confidence\": 0.7,\n          \"why\": \"Primary — directly addresses the feasibility of the Safetyist's control-based strategy.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"saf-intentions-069\",\n          \"label\": \"Grounding AI Safety in Legal Compliance Rather Than Abstract Ethics\",\n          \"operationality\": 6,\n          \"why\": \"Supporting — highlights the difficulty of applying domestic legal mandates to globalized hardware.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relevant_commitments\": [\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Safetyist\",\n      \"commitment\": \"Hardware-level attestation is a necessary circuit breaker for catastrophic AI risks.\",\n      \"type\": \"asserted\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Accelerationist\",\n      \"commitment\": \"Decentralized cryptographic verification can achieve safety without centralized master keys.\",\n      \"type\": \"asserted\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"edge_tensions\": [\n    {\n      \"edge\": \"The tension between 'safety-gated' deployment and the reality of globalized, open-source hardware.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Safetyist can exploit this by arguing that without a common hardware baseline, 'decentralized' audits are easily spoofed by malicious actors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"edge\": \"The 'regulatory-tech' gap: the potential for automated compliance to lower, rather than raise, barriers to entry.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Safetyist can pivot to this to counter the 'regulatory capture' argument by proposing that standardized hardware attestation could be an open-source, low-cost utility.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"phase_considerations\": \"The current phase demands a shift from ideological posturing to technical pragmatism. Safetyist must concede the difficulty of total supply chain control while arguing that a 'minimum viable safety baseline' at the hardware level is a necessary, if imperfect, defense against existential risk.\"\n}\n\n=== YOUR ARGUMENT PLAN ===\n{\n  \"strategic_goal\": \"Shift the debate from the false dichotomy of 'stagnation vs. speed' to the necessity of a standardized, low-barrier hardware attestation protocol that prevents catastrophic failure without centralizing control.\",\n  \"planned_moves\": [\n    {\n      \"move\": \"CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-13\",\n      \"detail\": \"Acknowledge that centralized oversight can lead to incumbent capture, then pivot to argue that open-source, hardware-level attestation standards actually lower barriers to entry by providing a universal, non-proprietary safety baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"SPECIFY\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-17\",\n      \"detail\": \"Address the infeasibility of total supply chain control by proposing a 'minimum viable safety baseline' focused on high-compute clusters rather than the entire global semiconductor market.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"INTEGRATE\",\n      \"detail\": \"Synthesize the need for decentralized cryptographic verification with the requirement for a hardware-level 'circuit breaker' to ensure that even decentralized systems have an emergency stop mechanism.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"target_claims\": [\n    \"AN-13\",\n    \"AN-17\"\n  ],\n  \"argument_sketch\": \"I will concede that poorly designed regulation risks incumbent capture, then demonstrate that a standardized, open-source hardware attestation protocol functions as a public utility rather than a gatekeeper. By narrowing the scope from 'total supply chain control' to 'high-compute cluster attestation,' I will show that a verifiable safety baseline is technically achievable. I will close by asserting that without this shared, immutable hardware-level circuit breaker, any decentralized audit is merely a post-hoc analysis of a catastrophe that has already occurred.\",\n  \"anticipated_responses\": [\n    \"Accelerationist will argue that any hardware-level requirement is inherently a 'chokepoint' for state control.\",\n    \"Skeptic will argue that high-compute clusters can be distributed globally to evade localized hardware mandates.\"\n  ],\n  \"target_nodes\": [\n    \"saf-intentions-211\",\n    \"cc-137\",\n    \"sit-032\",\n    \"saf-intentions-069\"\n  ]\n}\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- saf-intentions-211\n- cc-137\n- sit-032\n- saf-intentions-069\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxonomy_refs.\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nAddress Accelerationist on this point: Address the specific feasibility of enforcing hardware-level attestation on foreign-based chip foundries (AN-14) without triggering the 'regulatory capture' (AN-18) that your opponents argue is an inevitable consequence of Section 402.\n\nProbe deeper. Find cruxes, test edge cases, and name areas of agreement explicitly.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your debate statement following the plan's structure and moves. Stay in character as Safetyist.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n. Each paragraph develops one distinct idea.\n- A single unbroken block will be rejected — structure your argument into clear, quotable sections.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- ATTRIBUTION FIDELITY: You may only attribute positions to other debaters that they have explicitly stated in the debate history. Do not infer, extrapolate, or fabricate positions. Phrases like \"your solution is X\" or \"you're arguing for Y\" must correspond to something actually said — not an implication you've constructed. Misrepresenting another debater's position undermines the debate's integrity and will be flagged.\n- TOPIC PREMISE FIDELITY: Structural features stated in the debate topic are givens, not claims. Critique their consequences, not their existence.\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface AN-IDs or taxonomy node IDs in your statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence and note which prior claims it engages with.\n- TURN SYMBOLS: Choose 1-3 Unicode symbols (emoji) that capture your argument's essence. Tooltip: 1-sentence analogy connecting the symbol to your argument.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n\n=== FIELD-LEVEL FREEZE (from prior accepted draft) ===\nThe following fields passed validation. Copy them EXACTLY into your response — do not modify them:\n{\n  \"statement\": \"Centralized regulatory frameworks often invite capture by incumbents, yet the alternative of unconstrained development is not a free market but a race toward systemic fragility. We can mitigate this by shifting our focus from total supply chain control to a standardized, open-source hardware attestation protocol that functions as a public utility. By adopting a minimum viable safety baseline—specifically for high-compute clusters exceeding defined performance thresholds—regulators can provide an immutable circuit breaker without dictating the software or the business model. This approach avoids the pitfalls of proprietary gatekeeping while establishing a shared, verifiable foundation for safety that all participants must honor.\\n\\nTotal sovereignty over the global semiconductor supply chain is indeed infeasible, as noted by the 79% of respondents in \\\"The State of Sovereign AI: Exploring the Role of Open Source Projects and Global Collaboration in Global AI Strategy\\\" who identify sovereign AI as a complex, multi-layered strategic priority. We should accept this reality and narrow the regulatory scope to the specific hardware nodes where irreversible, catastrophic risks originate. By focusing on hardware-level attestation for top-tier compute clusters, we leverage existing administrative law mechanisms, similar to the risk-based obligations emphasized in \\\"Beyond Personhood: The Evolution of Legal Personhood and Its Implications for AI Recognition,\\\" to ensure accountability where it matters most.\\n\\nDecentralized cryptographic verification is a powerful tool, but it remains a post-hoc audit of a failure that has already occurred if it lacks a hardware-level emergency stop. A hardware-level circuit breaker creates a common, non-negotiable boundary that ensures human control remains the final arbiter of any system. Without this anchor, we rely on the hope that decentralized actors will voluntarily halt their own creation in the face of an emergent catastrophe. We must build institutions that prioritize the survival of the system over the speed of the individual components, or we will eventually face a failure that no amount of post-hoc auditing can reverse.\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"⚖️\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"A balance between the necessity of safety guardrails and the prevention of incumbent-driven regulatory capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🛑\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"The hardware-level circuit breaker that serves as the final, non-negotiable anchor for human control.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nOnly regenerate these fields: claim_sketches\nAll other fields above must appear verbatim in your output.\n\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"The Center for AI Standards and Innovation (CAISI) was established to serve as the primary industry point of contact within the U.S. government for AI testing and research.\"\n      — \"Center for AI Standards and Innovation (CAISI) | NIST\" (2025-06) → CAISI Role Establishment\n  [2] \"Violations of the Act that are determined to be uncurable are subject to civil penalties between $80,000 and $200,000.\"\n      — \"capitol.texas.gov tlodocs 89R analysis html HB00149S.htm\" (2026-01-01) → Uncurable Violation Penalty\n  [3] \"79% of survey respondents consider sovereign AI a strategic priority, with 66% identifying it as relevant at the national level and 47% at the organizational level.\"\n      — \"The State of Sovereign AI: Exploring the Role of Open Source Projects and Global Collaboration in Global AI Strategy\" (2025) → Sovereign AI Strategic Priority Statistics\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The authors support a 'legal construction of technology' method that prioritizes using existing legal institutions to perform meaning-making toward specific normative goals. This approach treats AI as a sociotechnical artifact that must be aligned wi\n      Quote: \"The law makes meaning of the social uses of technology—it constructs them. That’s not to say that’s all the law does. Law is construction with consequences. It conducts sensemaking through language, t\"\n      — \"Constructing AI Speech\" (safetyist, aligned)\n  [5] The document supports the EU's pivot toward a risk-based framework under the AI Act, which emphasizes ex ante compliance, transparency, and auditability. This aligns with the safetyist preference for using existing regulatory tools to enforce safety \n      Quote: \"In the wake of the Directive’s withdrawal, the European Commission’s strategy now favours extending risk-based obligations through the AI Act (COM(2021) 206 final), emphasising system classification, \"\n      — \"Beyond Personhood: The Evolution of Legal Personhood and Its Implications for AI Recognition\" (safetyist, aligned)\n\nYou MUST cite at least one source from the evidence above by title in your statement.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"Center for AI Standards and Innovation (CAISI) | NIST\" — NIST AI 800-4 identifies current practices and challenges in monitoring deployed AI systems based on practitioner workshops and a literature review.\n2. \"capitol.texas.gov tlodocs 89R analysis html HB00149S.htm\" — The Texas Artificial Intelligence Council is composed of seven members appointed by state officials.\n3. \"The State of Sovereign AI: Exploring the Role of Open Source Projects and Global Collaboration in Global AI Strategy\" — 93% of survey respondents agree that open collaboration is essential for building secure and culturally aligned sovereign AI systems.\n4. \"Constructing AI Speech\" — OpenAI transitioned from a nonprofit entity to a 'capped profit' structure with significant investment from Microsoft.\n5. \"Beyond Personhood: The Evolution of Legal Personhood and Its Implications for AI Recognition\" (doi:10.71265/ssvg8a97) — The US Federal Circuit ruled in Thaler v Vidal (2022) that AI cannot qualify as a legal agent for intellectual property rights because inventorship requires human intentionality.\n6. \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (2025) — A significant minority of both the general public (30%) and AI experts (24%) believe it is extremely or very likely that AI will make humans happier in the next 20 years.\n7. \"The Chinese Room Argument\" — John Searle's Chinese Room argument posits that syntax is not sufficient for semantics, meaning computer programs cannot produce minds.\n8. \"Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence\" (doi:10.1080/13600834.2020.1861714, 2020) — Legal personhood is not a monolithic concept; it is historically and culturally variable, often evolving based on changing social values and the necessity of property ownership.\n9. \"An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security\" — AI models have already demonstrated the capability to identify novel real-world vulnerabilities, such as in the context of cyberattacks.\n10. \"Natural Selection Favors AIs over Humans\" — Worldwide corporate investment in AI grew by 636% between 2015 and 2021, reaching $93.5 billion.\n11. \"The Philosophy of Speed: A Genealogy of Accelerationism\" — The e/acc movement identifies Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) as the key to unlocking a post-scarcity utopian future.\n12. \"Generative Privacy Doctrine: The Case For A New Legal Privacy Framework For Gen^Ai\" — Generative AI models are trained on massive datasets scraped from public websites and social media without individualized consent.\n13. \"The Role of AI Decision-Making for Land-Based Operations\" — AI systems in military contexts can lead to discriminatory outcomes or mistaken targets, including the wrongful targeting of civilians.\n14. \"Transhumanism\" — Whole brain emulation involves scanning a brain's structure to create a software model that replicates the original's behavior on appropriate hardware.\n15. \"Extreme power concentration   80,000 Hours\" — The Khmer Rouge regime murdered a quarter of all Cambodians between 1975 and 1979.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your full d