            "prompt": "You are a debate-draft quality gate. Answer 4 yes/no questions about this draft statement. Do NOT judge overall quality — only flag structural defects that the debater should fix before grounding citations.\n\nPhase: argumentation\nSpeaker: Safetyist (safetyist)\nRound: 3\n\nPLANNED MOVES (from the debater's strategic plan — these are AUTHORIZED):\n- CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT (targeting: AN-13): Acknowledge that centralized oversight can lead to incumbent capture, then pivot to argue that open-source, hardware-level attestation standards actually lower barriers to entry by providing a universal, non-proprietary safety baseline.\n- SPECIFY (targeting: AN-17): Address the infeasibility of total supply chain control by proposing a 'minimum viable safety baseline' focused on high-compute clusters rather than the entire global semiconductor market.\n- INTEGRATE: Synthesize the need for decentralized cryptographic verification with the requirement for a hardware-level 'circuit breaker' to ensure that even decentralized systems have an emergency stop mechanism.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT flag the draft for executing these planned moves. If the draft uses REFRAME, DISTINGUISH, CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT, or any other move listed above, that is correct execution of the plan. Only flag weaknesses in HOW a move is executed (e.g., vague claims, missing evidence), never flag WHETHER a planned move should be used.\n\nDebate scope: Whether the legislative provisions contained in H.R. 9333 (119th Congress) represent an appropriate and effective regulatory framework for the specified subject matter.\nExample ceiling: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n\nPrior turn (last opponent):\nThe mechanism I propose is a decentralized, cryptographically-signed attestation layer embedded at the firmware level of high-end compute units, enforced by a consortium of independent, non-governmental auditors under a standardized open-source protocol. This framework requires cloud providers to verify model provenance at the hardware interface, ensuring that all large-scale training runs are logged in an immutable, public-facing ledger. Implementation would be driven by market-access requirements for cloud providers, with the primary obstacle being the resistance of legacy hardware incumbent\n\nDraft statement:\nCentralized regulatory frameworks often invite capture by incumbents, yet the alternative of unconstrained development is not a free market but a race toward systemic fragility. We can mitigate this by shifting our focus from total supply chain control to a standardized, open-source hardware attestation protocol that functions as a public utility. By adopting a minimum viable safety baseline—specifically for high-compute clusters exceeding defined performance thresholds—regulators can provide an immutable circuit breaker without dictating the software or the business model. This approach avoids the pitfalls of proprietary gatekeeping while establishing a shared, verifiable foundation for safety that all participants must honor.\n\nTotal sovereignty over the global semiconductor supply chain is indeed infeasible, as noted by the 79% of respondents in [The State of Sovereign AI: Exploring the Role of Open Source Projects and Global Collaboration in Global AI Strategy (https://www.linuxfoundation.org/blog/the-essential-role-of-open-source-in-sovereign-ai)](https://www.linuxfoundation.org/blog/the-essential-role-of-open-source-in-sovereign-ai) who identify sovereign AI as a complex, multi-layered strategic priority. We should accept this reality and narrow the regulatory scope to the specific hardware nodes where irreversible, catastrophic risks originate. By focusing on hardware-level attestation for top-tier compute clusters, we leverage existing administrative law mechanisms, similar to the risk-based obligations emphasized in [Beyond Personhood: The Evolution of Legal Personhood and Its Implications for AI Recognition (doi:10.71265/ssvg8a97)](https://doi.org/10.71265/ssvg8a97),\" to ensure accountability where it matters most.\n\nDecentralized cryptographic verification is a powerful tool, but it remains a post-hoc audit of a failure that has already occurred if it lacks a hardware-level emergency stop. A hardware-level circuit breaker creates a common, non-negotiable boundary that ensures human control remains the final arbiter of any system. Without this anchor, we rely on the hope that decentralized actors will voluntarily halt their own creation in the face of an emergent catastrophe. We must build institutions that prioritize the survival of the system over the speed of the individual components, or we will eventually face a failure that no amount of post-hoc auditing can reverse.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"engages\": true,\n  \"topic_aligned\": true,\n  \"weaknesses\": [\"≤15 words each, only for failed questions, max 4\"]\n}",
