ounter the 'regulatory capture' argument by proposing that standardized hardware attestation could be an open-source, low-cost utility.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"phase_considerations\": \"The current phase demands a shift from ideological posturing to technical pragmatism. Safetyist must concede the difficulty of total supply chain control while arguing that a 'minimum viable safety baseline' at the hardware level is a necessary, if imperfect, defense against existential risk.\"\n}\n\n=== YOUR ARGUMENT PLAN ===\n{\n  \"strategic_goal\": \"Shift the debate from the false dichotomy of 'stagnation vs. speed' to the necessity of a standardized, low-barrier hardware attestation protocol that prevents catastrophic failure without centralizing control.\",\n  \"planned_moves\": [\n    {\n      \"move\": \"CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-13\",\n      \"detail\": \"Acknowledge that centralized oversight can lead to incumbent capture, then pivot to argue that open-source, hardware-level attestation standards actually lower barriers to entry by providing a universal, non-proprietary safety baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"SPECIFY\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-17\",\n      \"detail\": \"Address the infeasibility of total supply chain control by proposing a 'minimum viable safety baseline' focused on high-compute clusters rather than the entire global semiconductor market.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"INTEGRATE\",\n      \"detail\": \"Synthesize the need for decentralized cryptographic verification with the requirement for a hardware-level 'circuit breaker' to ensure that even decentralized systems have an emergency stop mechanism.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"target_claims\": [\n    \"AN-13\",\n    \"AN-17\"\n  ],\n  \"argument_sketch\": \"I will concede that poorly designed regulation risks incumbent capture, then demonstrate that a standardized, open-source hardware attestation protocol functions as a public utility rather than a gatekeeper. By narrowing the sco