ent, non-governmental auditors under a standardized open-source protocol. This framework requires cloud providers to verify model provenance at the hardware interface, ensuring that all large-scale training runs are logged in an immutable, public-facing ledger. Implementation would be driven by market-access requirements for cloud providers, with the primary obstacle being the resistance of legacy hardware incumbent\n\nDraft statement:\nSection 402 of H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for hardware-level attestation that prevents unrecoverable systemic failure. While critics argue this framework risks incumbent capture, a standardized, open-source attestation protocol functions as a public utility that lowers barriers to entry by replacing opaque, proprietary safety checks with a transparent, verifiable standard. By focusing this mandate on high-compute clusters exceeding 10^26 floating-point operations, regulators can ensure safety without requiring control over the entire global semiconductor supply chain.\n\nRegulatory capture often thrives on complexity and proprietary barriers. If we codify open-source attestation as a mandatory hardware baseline, we prevent dominant firms from using 'safety' as a shield to hide their own internal failure modes. This approach mirrors the structural requirements seen in aviation safety, where the 1958 Federal Aviation Act mandated uniform technical standards that compelled all manufacturers to meet a common, non-negotiable threshold for airworthiness. We are not choosing between stagnation and speed; we are choosing between controlled innovation and the irreversible hazards of unverified, high-compute deployment.\n\nDecentralized cryptographic verification is a useful audit tool, but it cannot replace the need for an emergency circuit breaker. A post-hoc audit provides data on a catastrophe that has already occurred, whereas hardware-level attestation provides the authority to halt an runaway process before it causes widespread harm. We must accept that absolute control over global foundries is infeasible, but we can secure the high-compute clusters where frontier-model training occurs. Without this shared, immutable hardware-level stop mechanism, any decentralized audit system remains a performance, not a safeguard.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"engages\": true,\n  \"topic_aligned\": true,\n  \"weaknesses\": [\"≤15 words each, only for failed questions, max 4\"]\n}",
