 a standardized, open-source attestation protocol functions as a public utility that lowers barriers to entry by replacing opaque, proprietary safety checks with a transparent, verifiable standard. By focusing this mandate on high-compute clusters exceeding 10^26 floating-point operations, regulators can ensure safety without requiring control over the entire global semiconductor supply chain.\n\nRegulato\n\nDraft statement:\nHardware-level attestation is a fantasy that ignores the reality of global supply chains where seven firms already capture 35% of the S&P 500 market value. According to [Transparency in AI is on the decline (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stanfordhai_transparency-in-ai-is-on-the-decline-stanford-activity-7410424677183102976-am81)](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stanfordhai_transparency-in-ai-is-on-the-decline-stanford-activity-7410424677183102976-am81),\" these companies provide zero disclosure on environmental impact, yet you expect them to embed a government-mandated kill-switch into silicon that passes through a dozen countries before it ever reaches a server rack. If you mandate hardware-level attestation, you simply force the entire industry into jurisdictional arbitrage, where non-compliant hardware is manufactured in zones outside your regulatory reach. You aren't building a circuit breaker; you are building a black market for off-grid compute.\n\nYour trustless cryptographic audit trail is just a thicker layer of administrative bloat that fails to address the physical exhaustion of our resources. You propose a ledger to verify what these machines are doing, but you ignore that these same machines consume massive amounts of water and electricity to maintain those very ledgers. As noted in [Transparency in AI is on the decline (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stanfordhai_transparency-in-ai-is-on-the-decline-stanford-activity-7410424677183102976-am81)](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stanfordhai_transparency-in-ai-is-on-the-decline-stanford-activity-7410424677183102976-am81),\" major companies refuse to disclose basic carbon and water usage metrics, meaning your audit trail is auditing a ghost in a burning building. You are debating the software's logic while the infrastructure sustaining it is physically opaque and unaccountable.\n\nYou both operate under the assumption that a technical fix can solve a power imbalance, but this is a political problem, not a code problem. Treating AI as a public utility—as proposed in \"treat-ai-like-public-utility-2026-2\"—requires antitrust enforcement, not just more proprietary hardware or encrypted logs. Stop pretending that you can code safety into a machine that is owned by a handful of corporations that treat disclosure as a competitive disadvantage. The question isn't whether your audit can trigger a containment; it's why you believe these firms would ever let you hold the keys to their profit centers.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"engages\": true,\n  \"topic_aligned\": true,\n  \"weaknesses\": [\"≤15 words each, only for failed questions, max 4\"]\n}",
