  "commitments": {
    "safetyist": {
      "asserted": [
        "H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems.",
        "By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.",
        "If a developer cannot demonstrate how to safely cease operation of their model, they have no legitimate claim to deploy it in our public systems.",
        "H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider liability to address the accountability gap inherent in deploying systems that can already outperform 94% of human experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.",
        "Operational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior.",
        "The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.",
        "By embedding safety checks at the silicon level, we move from reactive, software-only patches to a proactive, verifiable containment layer.",
        "Distributed Cryptographic Auditing serves as a valuable forensic ledger, but it is fundamentally incapable of preventing the irreversible harms described in the International AI Safety Report 2026.",
        "Section 402 of H.R. 9333 proposes an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities that acts as a circuit breaker.",
        "Relying solely on retrospective logs is a post-mortem strategy, not a safety policy.",
        "By focusing this mandate on high-compute clusters exceeding 10^26 floating-point operations, regulators can ensure safety without requiring control over the entire global semiconductor supply chain.",
        "If we codify open-source attestation as a mandatory hardware baseline, we prevent dominant firms from using 'safety' as a shield to hide their own internal failure modes."
      ],
      "conceded": [],
      "challenged": [
        "Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.",
        "Operational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control.",
        "The mandate for kill-switches is a technical fantasy that fails to account for the proliferation of local, edge-deployed AI models.",
        "The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc.",
        "Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.",
        "Hardware-level attestation is a fantasy that forces the semiconductor industry into a state-sanctioned oligopoly while ignoring the reality of distributed innovation."
      ]
    },
    "accelerationist": {
      "asserted": [
        "H.R. 9333 is a structural mandate for institutional stagnation that sacrifices our competitive edge for the illusion of domestic control.",
        "Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.",
        "Operational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control.",
        "Hardware-level attestation is a fantasy that forces the semiconductor industry into a state-sanctioned oligopoly while ignoring the reality of distributed innovation.",
        "Mandating hardware-level kill-switches ignores the existence of open-source silicon and fragmented global supply chains, which will simply route around your domestic mandates.",
        "We should prioritize building the physical capacity to out-innovate threats, rather than wasting $670 billion in 2026-era infrastructure spending on 'theater of control' mechanisms that provide no real security.",
        "Mandating proprietary 'kill switches' within chip architecture effectively hands the keys to the U.S. CHIPS and Science Act’s $630 billion investment recipients, creating a moat that prevents smaller, open-source innovators from entering the market.",
        "My proposal leverages existing cryptographic standards to create a 'trustless' audit trail that allows for rapid intervention without a government-held master key.",
        "The PRC’s $47.5 billion fund for semiconductor self-sufficiency already makes unilateral U.S. control a strategic impossibility."
      ],
      "conceded": [
        "The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc."
      ],
      "challenged": [
        "H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems.",
        "By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.",
        "The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.",
        "By embedding safety checks at the silicon level, we move from reactive, software-only patches to a proactive, verifiable containment layer.",
        "Operational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior."
      ]
    },
    "skeptic": {
      "asserted": [
        "H.R. 9333 is a regulatory moat that entrenches incumbents by offloading liability onto cloud providers.",
        "The mandate for kill-switches is a technical fantasy that fails to account for the proliferation of local, edge-deployed AI models.",
        "By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.",
        "Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.",
        "The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc.",
        "Ten major AI companies currently disclose zero information regarding their energy usage or environmental impact, proving that these firms will resist any transparency.",
        "Your trustless cryptographic audit trail is just a thicker layer of administrative bloat that fails to address the physical exhaustion of our resources.",
        "Treating AI as a public utility requires antitrust enforcement, not just more proprietary hardware or encrypted logs."
      ],
      "conceded": [
        "H.R. 9333 is a structural mandate for institutional stagnation that sacrifices our competitive edge for the illusion of domestic control.",
        "Operational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control.",
        "Mandating hardware-level kill-switches ignores the existence of open-source silicon and fragmented global supply chains, which will simply route around your domestic mandates."
      ],
      "challenged": [
        "By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.",
        "The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.",
        "My proposal leverages existing cryptographic standards to create a 'trustless' audit trail that allows for rapid intervention without a government-held master key.",
        "Section 402 of H.R. 9333 proposes an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities that acts as a circuit breaker."
      ]
    }
