      {
        "id": "AN-6",
        "text": "Fact-check (disputed): The claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.",
