  "unanswered_claims_ledger": [
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-2",
      "claim_text": "By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 2,
      "addressed_round": 3,
      "addressed_by": "accelerationist"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-4",
      "claim_text": "Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 3,
      "addressed_round": 6,
      "addressed_by": "system"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-6",
      "claim_text": "Fact-check (disputed): The claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.",
      "speaker": "system",
      "first_unanswered_round": 6
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-9",
      "claim_text": "H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider liability to address the accountability gap inherent in deploying systems that can already outperform 94% of human experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 8
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-10",
      "claim_text": "Operational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 8,
      "addressed_round": 11,
      "addressed_by": "accelerationist"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-11",
      "claim_text": "The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 10,
      "addressed_round": 11,
      "addressed_by": "accelerationist"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-12",
      "claim_text": "By embedding safety checks at the silicon level, we move from reactive, software-only patches to a proactive, verifiable containment layer.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 10,
      "addressed_round": 11,
      "addressed_by": "accelerationist"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-14",
      "claim_text": "Mandating hardware-level kill-switches ignores the existence of open-source silicon and fragmented global supply chains, which will simply route around your domestic mandates.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 11,
      "addressed_round": 15,
      "addressed_by": "skeptic"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-15",
      "claim_text": "We should prioritize building the physical capacity to out-innovate threats, rather than wasting $670 billion in 2026-era infrastructure spending on 'theater of control' mechanisms that provide no real security.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 11
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-16",
      "claim_text": "By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.",
      "speaker": "skeptic",
      "first_unanswered_round": 15,
      "addressed_round": 19,
      "addressed_by": "system"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-17",
      "claim_text": "Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.",
      "speaker": "skeptic",
      "first_unanswered_round": 15,
      "addressed_round": 19,
      "addressed_by": "system"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-18",
      "claim_text": "Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately identifies a documented structural risk where high compliance costs consolidate market power among incumbents. While 'tech royalty' is a descriptive term rather than a formal legal definition, the economic phenomenon of regulatory capture—where small firms rely on large-vendor infrastructure to meet government mandates—is a well-supported concern in current AI policy analysis.",
      "speaker": "system",
      "first_unanswered_round": 19
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-19",
      "claim_text": "Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately cites the Brookings Institution's February 2026 report, which concludes that total national control over the AI supply chain is structurally impossible. The report identifies global interdependencies across minerals, compute, and data as insurmountable barriers to full-stack sovereignty.",
      "speaker": "system",
      "first_unanswered_round": 19
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-20",
      "claim_text": "The mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc.",
      "speaker": "skeptic",
      "first_unanswered_round": 19,
      "addressed_round": 20,
      "addressed_by": "safetyist"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-21",
      "claim_text": "Distributed Cryptographic Auditing serves as a valuable forensic ledger, but it is fundamentally incapable of preventing the irreversible harms described in the International AI Safety Report 2026.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 20,
      "addressed_round": 23,
      "addressed_by": "system"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-22",
      "claim_text": "Section 402 of H.R. 9333 proposes an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities that acts as a circuit breaker.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 20,
      "addressed_round": 27,
      "addressed_by": "skeptic"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-23",
      "claim_text": "Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately distinguishes between detective and preventative security controls. Distributed Cryptographic Auditing functions as a forensic ledger for accountability, but it lacks the real-time intervention capabilities required to stop autonomous systems from executing irreversible harms.",
      "speaker": "system",
      "first_unanswered_round": 23
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-24",
      "claim_text": "Mandating proprietary 'kill switches' within chip architecture effectively hands the keys to the U.S. CHIPS and Science Act’s $630 billion investment recipients, creating a moat that prevents smaller, open-source innovators from entering the market.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 23
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-25",
      "claim_text": "My proposal leverages existing cryptographic standards to create a 'trustless' audit trail that allows for rapid intervention without a government-held master key.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 23,
      "addressed_round": 27,
      "addressed_by": "skeptic"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-26",
      "claim_text": "The PRC’s $47.5 billion fund for semiconductor self-sufficiency already makes unilateral U.S. control a strategic impossibility.",
      "speaker": "accelerationist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 23,
      "addressed_round": 26,
      "addressed_by": "system"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-27",
      "claim_text": "Fact-check (disputed): The claim that China's $47.5 billion 'Big Fund' Phase III makes U.S. control a strategic impossibility is an exaggeration of current technological realities. While the fund represents a massive state-backed effort to overcome 'chokepoints'—critical manufacturing bottlenecks like lithography—independent analysts confirm that U.S. export controls still successfully delay China's access to advanced fabrication nodes. The strategic efficacy of these controls remains a matter of ongoing debate rather than a settled impossibility.",
      "speaker": "system",
      "first_unanswered_round": 26
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-28",
      "claim_text": "We only require that the final integration of high-compute hardware in the United States triggers a verifiable safety handshake.",
      "speaker": "safetyist",
      "first_unanswered_round": 26
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-30",
      "claim_text": "Your trustless cryptographic audit trail is just a thicker layer of administrative bloat that fails to address the physical exhaustion of our resources.",
      "speaker": "skeptic",
      "first_unanswered_round": 27
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "AN-31",
      "claim_text": "Treating AI as a public utility requires antitrust enforcement, not just more proprietary hardware or encrypted logs.",
      "speaker": "skeptic",
      "first_unanswered_round": 27
    }
  ],
