g market forces.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim-3\",\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"claim_text\": \"Once an open-weight model is downloaded to a local server, it exists outside the reach of any API-based kill-switch.\",\n      \"neutral_assessment\": \"well_supported\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a logical observation regarding the nature of distributed software, which Speaker C implicitly concedes by pivoting to hardware-level attestation as a solution.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim-4\",\n      \"speaker\": \"C\",\n      \"claim_text\": \"Hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers, creates a physical kill-switch for unauthorized model execution.\",\n      \"neutral_assessment\": \"plausible_but_underdefended\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"While technically possible in theory, the speaker does not address the practical challenges of global supply chain integrity or the potential for foreign adversaries to bypass these controls.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claim-5\",\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"claim_text\": \"Mandating hardware-level backdoors creates a single point of failure that foreign adversaries will reverse-engineer.\",\n      \"neutral_assessment\": \"plausible_but_underdefended\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The speaker provides a logical security argument regarding backdoors, but lacks specific evidence that such a mandate would be the primary vector for foreign exploitation.\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_assessment\": {\n    \"strongest_unaddressed_claim_id\": \"claim-4\",\n    \"debate_is_engaging_real_disagreement\": true,\n    \"notes\": \"The speakers are engaging in a substantive disagreement regarding the trade-offs between centralized safety mandates and decentralized innovation. While the debate has moved from high-level rhetoric to specific technical mechanisms like h