              "prompt": "You are a specificity editor. Add concrete facts to abstract claims.\n\n=== STATEMENT TO FIX ===\nKill-switches are not binary theater; they are circuit breakers for the digital age, analogous to the emergency stop buttons mandated in industrial manufacturing or the automated trading halts on the New York Stock Exchange. When an AI agent demonstrates capabilities that exceed human intervention speeds—such as the performance documented in the 'international ai safety report 2026 1,' where models outperformed 94% of experts in virology protocols—the absence of a physical disconnect creates an unrecoverable systemic risk. We cannot rely on software-layer overrides when the underlying system has already exceeded the cognitive capacity of its human overseers. An effective kill-switch forces a state of pause that allows for human re-evaluation, preventing the rapid propagation of catastrophic errors.\n\nCloud-provider liability is the only mechanism that forces accountability onto the entities profiting from the deployment of high-risk, unverified models. Critics claim this creates a chilling effect on open-source development, yet they ignore that anonymous, large-scale compute deployment creates a permanent vacuum of responsibility for irreversible harms. If a model’s weights are public but its behavior is unpredictable, as noted in 'AI Safety Is a Category Error,' the developer who released the weights has effectively abandoned the duty of care. Mandating that providers verify the safety profile of high-compute models is not an innovation tax; it is a standard cost of operation for any technology that threatens public safety, similar to the environmental and liability standards enforced under the Clean Air Act.\n\nWe must shift the burden of proof to those who argue for unconstrained deployment. If you maintain that open-source models are inherently safe, you must present a concrete, verifiable rollback mechanism for when those models cause harm at scale. The current reliance on 'decentralized resilience' is a failure to acknowledge that decentralized systems without accountability are merely unmanageable sources of systemic chaos. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable; on what authority do you claim we can remove them without inviting collapse?\n\n=== CLAIMS THAT NEED SPECIFICS (flagged as too abstract) ===\n1. \"Kill-switches are not binary theater; they are circuit breakers for the digital age, analogous to the emergency stop buttons mandated in industrial manufacturing.\"\n2. \"Cloud-provider liability is the only mechanism that forces accountability onto the entities profiting from the deployment of high-risk, unverified models.\"\n3. \"If you maintain that open-source models are inherently safe, you must present a concrete, verifiable rollback mechanism for when those models cause harm at scale.\"\n\n=== FACTS YOU CAN CITE ===\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy (RSP) defines AI Safety Levels (ASL) to manage catastrophic risks, modeled after US government biosafety levels.\"\n      — \"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy\" (2026-02) → ASL Framework Definition\n  [2] \"An AI agent identified 77% of vulnerabilities in real software during a premier cyber competition, placing it in the top 5% of over 400 teams.\"\n      — \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (2025) → AI Vulnerability Discovery Performance\n  [3] \"Training a single moderately large model produces twice the American lifetime's worth of CO2.\"\n      — \"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\" (2024-07) → Environmental Cost of Training\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The docu