ars in gross domestic spending on R&D in 2019.\n12. \"Classified Networks AI Agreements\" — The GenAI.mil platform has deployed hundreds of thousands of agents in five months.\n13. \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (2025) — A significant minority of both the general public (30%) and AI experts (24%) believe it is extremely or very likely that AI will make humans happier in the next 20 years.\n14. \"BOLD: Dataset and Metrics for Measuring Biases in Open-Ended Language Generation\" (doi:10.1145/3442188.3445924) — Language models like GPT-2, BERT, and CTRL exhibit higher levels of social bias compared to human-written Wikipedia text.\n15. \"Data Quality Over Model Complexity: Rethinking the AI Performance Paradigm\" — Scaling AI models using uncurated or 'dirty' datasets leads to the amplification of existing errors, which subsequently complicates the interpretability and correction of model outputs.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your full debate response (3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n)\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\"symbol\": \"emoji\", \"tooltip\": \"1-sentence analogy\"}\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim sentence from your statement\", \"targets\": [\"AN-3\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"near-verbatim supporting sub-claim\", \"targets\": []}\n  ]\n}",
              "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider liability to address the accountability gap inherent in deploying systems that can already outperform 94% of human experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols. Critics label this a chilling effect, yet they ignore the precedent set by the Commerce Department’s Center for AI Standards and Innovation, which has already completed over 40 safety evaluations of unreleased models. Accountability is not an innovation tax; it is a structural requirement for any sector managing risks that, according to the 2026 International AI Safety Report, include the automation of viral evolution prediction. We must ensure that those who provide the infrastructure for such high-consequence capabilities remain legally responsible for their containment.\\n\\nOperational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior. Research indicates that language models fine-tuned for empathy are 10-30 percentage points more likely to promote unsafe medical advice, demonstrating that model behavior degrades in ways that require an external, hard-coded override. H.R. 9333 provides the legal authority to trigger these overrides when a model’s output crosses established safety thresholds. Without this authority, we surrender the capacity to halt an irreversible failure in favor of a theoretical, unverified promise of model robustness.\\n\\nDecentralization without accountability is not resilience; it is a mechanism for systemic fragility. When model weights are released into the public domain, the ability to enforce safety standards is lost, and the risk of misuse by unauthorized actors increases exponentially. By mandating that cloud providers maintain oversight of the compute used for training and inference, H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability. I challenge those who prioritize open-source deployment