          "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation enforced through secure enclaves, requiring manufacturers to integrate remote-disablement protocols into high-compute AI chips under Department of Commerce oversight. This architecture enables a verifiable link between regulatory authority and physical deployment, ensuring that models exceeding safety baselines remain under human control. We must treat these safeguards not as elective features, but as foundational components of civilizational infrastructure.\\n\\nCritics argue that edge-deployed models render centralized oversight a theater of control, yet this ignores the reality of supply chain dependencies. As documented in the \\\"International AI Safety Report 2026,\\\" AI models are already capable of performing complex biological tasks, meaning the risk of uncontrolled deployment is not a theoretical abstraction but a documented threat to public safety. By mandating that high-compute hardware verify its operational status against a secure, government-managed registry, we ensure that an off-switch remains a practical reality rather than a conceptual hope. This aligns with the public's stated desire for more control over AI, as noted in the \\\"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report,\\\" where only 14% of the general public feel they currently possess meaningful influence over these systems.\\n\\nLiability frameworks are the necessary filter for the systemic risks posed by unverified deployment. While some fear this will chill open-source development, we must recognize that accountability is the prerequisite for innovation that does not endanger the collective. If a model’s capabilities cross into the catastrophic, the developer must bear the financial and legal consequences of failing to implement the required safety protocols. This is not about stifling progress; it is about ensuring that those who profit from high-compute deployment are also the ones held accountable for the resulting societal instability.\\n\\nWe spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable, and we cannot afford to dismantle them for the sake of unconstrained deployment. The \\\"AI RMF Playbook\\\" highlights that systems in high-stakes settings require continuous monitoring because they perform in unexpected ways after release. On what authority do you claim we can remove these boundaries without inviting collapse? The burden of proof now rests upon those who would champion a world of zero-accountability hardware to explain how they intend to prevent the next irreversible failure.\",\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation enforced through secure enclaves, requiring manufacturers to integrate remote-disablement protocols into high-compute AI chips under Department of Commerce oversight.\",\n      \"targets\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"By mandating that high-compute hardware verify its operational status against a secure, government-managed registry, we ensure that an off-switch remains a practical reality rather than a conceptual hope.\",\n      \"targets\": [\"AN-8\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Liability frameworks are the necessary filter for the systemic risks posed by unverified deployment.\",\n      \"targets\": [\"AN-4\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"policy_challenge_response\": {\n    \"mechanism\": \"Hardware-level attestation via secure enclaves and mandatory remote-disablement protocols for high-compute AI hardware.\",\n    \"actor\": \"Hardware manufacturers and cloud providers, overseen by the Department of Commerce.\",\n    \"feasibility\": \"Moderate; supported by national security coalitions concerned with dual-use risks and biological threats, though opposed by segments of the open-source community.\",\n    \"obstacle\": \"The proliferation of legacy hardware and the technical difficulty of maintaining secure enclaves w