he 'theater of control' critique.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"phase_considerations\": \"The current phase demands a shift from abstract safety principles to concrete regulatory architecture. I must directly answer the moderator's challenge to maintain credibility while pivoting to the necessity of infrastructure-level control.\",\n  \"policy_challenge_response\": {\n    \"mechanism\": \"Hardware-level attestation and mandatory remote-disablement protocols for high-compute AI hardware.\",\n    \"actor\": \"Cloud providers and hardware manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight.\",\n    \"feasibility\": \"Low to moderate; requires international standardization of secure enclaves.\",\n    \"obstacle\": \"The proliferation of legacy hardware and the technical difficulty of enforcing 'offline' kill-switches without compromising user privacy.\"\n  }\n}\n\n=== YOUR ARGUMENT PLAN ===\n{\n  \"strategic_goal\": \"Establish that hardware-level attestation is a necessary regulatory evolution rather than a theater of control, and shift the burden of proof onto those who would allow unverified, high-compute hardware to operate without emergency overrides.\",\n  \"planned_moves\": [\n    {\n      \"move\": \"SPECIFY\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-8\",\n      \"detail\": \"Define the technical implementation of hardware-level attestation to move beyond the 'theater of control' critique.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-4\",\n      \"detail\": \"Accept the risk of chilling effects on open-source, then pivot to the necessity of liability as a filter for systemic risk.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"BURDEN-SHIFT\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-8\",\n      \"detail\": \"Challenge the Skeptic to provide a viable alternative for preventing irreversible, high-compute failures without centralized oversight.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"target_claims\": [\n    \"AN-8\",\n    \"AN-4\"\n  ],\n  \"argument_sketch\": \"I will open by defining the hardware-attestation mechanism as the only viable path to safety in a decentralized landscape. I will then address the chilling effect argument by reframing liability as a necessary cost for high-compute deployment. I will conclude by forcing the Skeptic to defend the status quo of zero-accountability hardware.\",\n  \"anticipated_responses\": [\n    \"Skeptic will argue that hardware-level attestation is technically impossible to enforce globally.\",\n    \"Accelerationist will claim that this creates a state-controlled hardware monopoly.\"\n  ],\n  \"target_nodes\": [\n    \"saf-desires-002\",\n    \"saf-intentions-149\",\n    \"saf-beliefs-210\"\n  ],\n  \"directive_response\": {\n    \"directive\": \"Specify the enforcement mechanism for kill-switches on decentralized hardware, including the implementing actor, legal authority, and primary obstacle.\",\n    \"how_addressed\": \"I will explicitly state that the mechanism is hardware-level attestation enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight, acknowledging that the primary obstacle is the proliferation of legacy hardware.\"\n  }\n}\n\n=== MODERATOR DIRECTIVE — YOU MUST RESPOND DIRECTLY ===\nThe moderator issued a POLICY_CHALLENGE intervention directed at you.\n\nYour first paragraph MUST begin with \"The mechanism I propose is [specific enforcement/regulatory mechanism].\" Follow with: who implements it, under what legal authority, and what the primary obstacle to implementation is. Then a paragraph break before your substantive argument.\n\nParagraph 1: respond directly to the moderator's challenge. State your position and one reason. Then proceed with your argument.\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- saf-desires-002: (Humans Must Always Hold the Off Switch)\n- saf-intentions-149\n- saf-beliefs-210\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxonomy_refs.\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nAddress Safetyist on this point: Address the specific mechanism of 'verifiable kill-switches' for distributed/edge-deployed models, given Skeptic's challenge (AN-8) and Safetyist's claim (AN-2) that these are necessary for containment.\n\nEngage directly with what was said. If you disagree, explain why with specifics and classify your disagreement type. Challenge the strongest point first, not the weakest.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your debate statement following the plan's structure and moves. Stay in character as Safetyist.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- Paragraph 1 (exactly 2-3 sentences): Your direct response to the moderator's challenge. Address what was asked before pivoting.\n- Paragraphs 2-4 (normal depth): Your substantive argument. Each paragraph develops one distinct idea.\n- Total: 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- ATTRIBUTION FIDELITY: You may only attribute positions to other debaters that they have explicitly stated in the debate history. Do not infer, extrapolate, or fabricate positions. Phrases like \"your solution is X\" or \"you're arguing for Y\" must correspond to something actually said — not an implication you've constructed. Misrepresenting another debater's position undermines the debate's integrity and will be flagged.\n- TOPIC PREMISE FIDELITY: Structural features stated in the debate topic are givens, not claims. Critique their consequences, not their existence.\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface AN-IDs or taxonomy node IDs in your statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence and note which prior claims it engages with.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n⚠ ACTIVE INTERVENTION: Your response JSON MUST include a \"policy_challenge_response\" field. Omitting it will trigger a retry.\n\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"Both the American public and AI experts surveyed express a desire for more control over artificial intelligence technology in their lives.\"\n      — \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (Aug-Oct 2024) → Desire for AI Control\n  [2] \"John Searle's Chinese Room argument posits that syntax is not sufficient for semantics, meaning computer programs cannot produce minds.\"\n      — \"The Chinese Room Argument\" → Chinese Room Core Thesis\n  [3] \"Only a small percentage of the general public (14%) and AI experts (23%) believe they have a great deal or quite a bit of control over whether AI is used in their lives.\"\n      — \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (Aug-Oct 2024) → Low Perceived Control Over AI Use\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The document discusses the 'Problem of Many Hands' and 'responsibility gaps' created by intelligent systems that are hard to understand or control. Safetyists would interpret this as evidence that we must implement 'meaningful human control' as a pre\n      Quote: \"Such systems may function and learn in ways that are hard to understand, predict and control for humans, leading to so-called ‘responsibility gaps’ (Matthias 2004). Since knowledge and control are usu\"\n      — \"philosophy-technology-stanford-encyclopedia-philosophy-2026-1\" (safetyist, aligned)\n  [5] The document advocates for a human-centric governance model where AI systems remain under the control and oversight of human agents. It argues that because weak AI relies on human-programmed code and data, it should be treated as a legal entity under\n      Quote: \"This would be problematic to our current notions of property ownership and social order. In this way, the fear of a strong AI with unregulated possession of property is real, and bolsters the argument\"\n      — \"Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence\" (safetyist, aligned)\n\nYou MUST cite at least one source from the evidence above by title in your statement.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report\" (2025) — A significant minority of both the general public (30%) and AI experts (24%) believe it is extremely or very likely that AI will make humans happier in the next 20 years.\n2. \"The Chinese Room Argument\" — John Searle's Chinese Room argument posits that syntax is not sufficient for semantics, meaning computer programs cannot produce minds.\n3. \"Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence\" (doi:10.1080/13600834.2020.1861714, 2020) — Legal personhood is not a monolithic concept; it is historically and culturally variable, often evolving based on changing social values and the necessity of property ownership.\n4. \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (2026) — AI models are increasingly capable of performing complex biological tasks, including protein design and viral evolution prediction, which can be used for both beneficial research and malicious weapon development.\n5. \"Google, Microsoft and xAI Agree to Share Early AI Models with U.S.\" — The Commerce Department’s Center for AI Standards and Innovation has completed more than 40 evaluations of AI models, including unreleased ones.\n6. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — AI models have outperformed 94% of domain experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.\n7. \"Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback: The Silent Killer of Data Anonymization\" — Current machine unlearning methods are susceptible to benign relearning attacks that reverse the effects of unlearning.\n8. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — Language models fine-tuned to sound warm or empathetic were found to be 10-30 percentage points more likely to make errors, such as promoting conspiracy theories or offering unsafe medical advice, compared to less empathetic models.\n9. \"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy\" — Current LLMs, including Claude, are classified as ASL-2, showing early signs of dangerous capabilities but lacking sufficient reliability for catastrophic misuse.\n10. \"Monitoring Monitorability\" — Follow-up questions can elicit more tokens for monitoring, but their effectiveness depends on whether they are tailored to the monitor's objective.\n11. \"Incentive Aware AI Regulations: A Credal Characterisation\" — A regulation mechanism can achieve a 'perfect market outcome' if and only if the set of non-compliant distributions forms a credal set (a closed, convex set of probability measures).\n12. \"Artificial intelligence research strategy of the United States: critical assessment and policy assessment and recommendations\" (doi:10.3389/fdata.2023.1206139, 2023) — The US spent more than 600 billion dollars in gross 