nes remain under human control. We must treat these safeguards not as elective features, but as foundational components of civilizational infrastructure.\n\nCritics argue that edge-deployed models render centralized oversight a theater of control, yet this ignores the reality of supply chain dependencies. As documented in the [International AI Safety Report 2026 (arXiv:2602.21012v1)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21012v1),\" AI models are already capable of performing complex biological tasks, meaning the risk of uncontrolled deployment is not a theoretical abstraction but a documented threat to public safety. By mandating that high-compute hardware verify its operational status against a secure, government-managed registry, we ensure that an off-switch remains a practical reality rather than a conceptual hope. This aligns with the public's stated desire for more control over AI, as noted in the [pi 2025.04.03 us public and ai experts report (https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2025/04/03/how-the-us-public-and-ai-experts-view-artificial-intelligence/)](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2025/04/03/how-the-us-public-and-ai-experts-view-artificial-intelligence/),\" where only 14% of the general public feel they currently possess meaningful influence over these systems.\n\nLiability frameworks are the necessary filter for the systemic risks posed by unverified deployment. While some fear this will chill open-source development, we must recognize that accountability is the prerequisite for innovation that does not endanger the collective. If a model’s capabilities cross into the catastrophic, the developer must bear the financial and legal consequences of failing to implement the required safety protocols. This is not about stifling progress; it is about ensuring that those who profit from high-compute deployment are also the ones held accountable for the resulting societal instability.\n\nWe spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable, and we cannot afford to dismantle them for the sake of unconstrained deployment. The [AI RMF Playbook (https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework/nist-ai-rmf-playbook)](https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework/nist-ai-rmf-playbook) highlights that systems in high-stakes settings require continuous monitoring because they perform in unexpected ways after release. On what authority do you claim we can remove these boundaries without inviting collapse? The burden of proof now rests upon those who would champion a world of zero-accountability hardware to explain how they intend to prevent the next irreversible failure.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose: