testation mechanism as the only viable path to safety in a decentralized landscape. I will then address the chilling effect argument by reframing liability as a necessary cost for high-compute deployment. I will conclude by forcing the Skeptic to defend the status quo of zero-accountability hardware.\",\n  \"anticipated_responses\": [\n    \"Skeptic will argue that hardware-level attestation is technically impossible to enforce globally.\",\n    \"Accelerationist will claim that this creates a state-controlled hardware monopoly.\"\n  ],\n  \"target_nodes\": [\n    \"saf-desires-002\",\n    \"saf-intentions-149\",\n    \"saf-beliefs-210\"\n  ],\n  \"directive_response\": {\n    \"directive\": \"Specify the enforcement mechanism for kill-switches on decentralized hardware, including the implementing actor, legal authority, and primary obstacle.\",\n    \"how_addressed\": \"I will explicitly state that the mechanism is hardware-level attestation enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight, acknowledging that the primary obstacle is the proliferation of legacy hardware.\"\n  }\n}\n\n=== MODERATOR DIRECTIVE — YOU MUST RESPOND DIRECTLY ===\nThe moderator issued a POLICY_CHALLENGE intervention directed at you.\n\nYour first paragraph MUST begin with \"The mechanism I propose is [specific enforcement/regulatory mechanism].\" Follow with: who implements it, under what legal authority, and what the primary obstacle to implementation is. Then a paragraph break before your substantive argument.\n\nParagraph 1: respond directly to the moderator's challenge. State your position and one reason. Then proceed with your argument.\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- saf-desires-002: (Humans Must Always Hold the Off Switch)\n- saf-intentions-149\n- saf-beliefs-210\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxono