k control is structurally infeasible for any single nation. When you force this architecture, you aren't creating a kill-switch for 'high-consequence AI'; you are creating a massive, brittle bottleneck that will inevitably break. If the Department of Commerce manages to force this through, who exactly is held liable when the inevitable breach of their attestation infrastructure exposes the entire national grid to control by an adversary?\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🔑\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"Hardware-level attestation functions as a universal master key that creates a catastrophic single point of failure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🏰\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"The regulatory framework acts as a moat, protecting incumbent tech giants from competition while offloading liability.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Hardware-level attestation is not a safety feature; it is a state-sanctioned master key that guarantees a single point of failure.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-11\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-14\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-14\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
