              "prompt": "You are a debate-draft quality gate. Answer 4 yes/no questions about this draft statement. Do NOT judge overall quality — only flag structural defects that the debater should fix before grounding citations.\n\nPhase: argumentation\nSpeaker: Skeptic (skeptic)\nRound: 3\n\nPLANNED MOVES (from the debater's strategic plan — these are AUTHORIZED):\n- DISTINGUISH (targeting: AN-11): Distinguish between state-controlled hardware 'kill-switches' and decentralized cryptographic auditability.\n- SPECIFY (targeting: AN-14): Detail how open-source silicon can support attestation without centralized authority.\n- INTEGRATE: Synthesize the need for safety with the material reality of distributed hardware supply chains.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT flag the draft for executing these planned moves. If the draft uses REFRAME, DISTINGUISH, CONCEDE-AND-PIVOT, or any other move listed above, that is correct execution of the plan. Only flag weaknesses in HOW a move is executed (e.g., vague claims, missing evidence), never flag WHETHER a planned move should be used.\n\nDebate scope: Whether the legislative provisions contained in H.R. 9333 (119th Congress) represent an appropriate and effective regulatory framework for the specified subject matter.\nExample ceiling: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n\nPrior turn (last opponent):\nHardware-level attestation is a fantasy that forces the semiconductor industry into a state-sanctioned oligopoly while ignoring the reality of distributed innovation. You are effectively proposing to turn every silicon chip into a state-monitored checkpoint, a move that would consolidate power in the hands of the three dominant global manufacturers. As noted in '[AI Chip Supply Chain Risk 2026: Your Essential Guide (https://enkiai.com/ai-market-intelligence/ai-chip-supply-chain-risk-2026-your-essential-guide/)](https://enkiai.com/ai-market-intelligence/ai-chip-supply-chain-risk-2026-your-essen\n\nDraft statement:\nThe mechanism I propose is a Distributed Cryptographic Audit (DCA) framework, implemented by hardware manufacturers via mandatory, standardized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc. This system, enforced by the Federal Trade Commission under existing consumer protection and unfair competition statutes, replaces the need for centralized government 'master keys' by focusing on verifiable operational transparency rather than pre-clearance. The primary obstacle to this implementation is the intense lobbying of incumbent firms that benefit from the opaque, proprietary nature of current hardware supply chains.\n\nHardware-level attestation as proposed in Section 402 is a fantasy that ignores the material reality of globalized supply chains. You cannot secure a silicon architecture through a centralized mandate when the design, fabrication, and packaging of chips occur across disparate jurisdictions, from Taiwan to Ireland. As noted in '[Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence (https://muckrack.com/cameron-f-kerry/articles)](https://muckrack.com/cameron-f-kerry/articles),' full-stack sovereignty is structurally infeasible, meaning any attempt to force a single, state-controlled 'kill-switch' into this web will simply be bypassed or ignored by global actors. This is not a security feature; it is a political chokepoint that prioritizes the centralization of control over the actual mitigation of cascading failures.\n\nInstead of chasing the illusion of a master kill-switch, we must adopt the principles of graceful degradation and circuit breakers described in '[AI Safety Is a Category Error (https://x.com/jsnover/status/2036887650146082967)](https://x.com/jsnover/status/2036887650146082967).' By requiring that all hardware devices output cryptographically signed logs of their operational status, we shift the burden to post-hoc audits where failures can be identified and mitigated in real-time. This provides the safety we need without creating the single point of failure that a government-held master key represents. Ten major AI companies currently disclose zero information regarding their energy usage or environmental impact, proving that these firms will resist any transparency that reveals their actual operational footprint; our job is to force that data into the light through standardized, auditable protocols.\n\nTrue regulatory effectiveness comes from making it impossible for companies to hide their systemic fragilities, not from giving them an off-switch they will never use. We have seen this movie before; when we allow incumbents to define the compliance standard, they pull up the drawbridge to protect their 35% stake in the S&P 500. A decentralized audit trail forces these firms to play by the same rules as the open-source community, ensuring that safety is a measurable standard rather than an abstract promise. Who is going to audit the auditors when the Department of Commerce is busy managing the keys to the kingdom?\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"engages\": true,\n  \"topic_aligned\": true,\n  \"weaknesses\": [\"≤15 words each, only for failed questions, max 4\"]\n}",
