ent vs. Post-hoc accountability\",\n      \"relevance\": \"The Safetyist must demonstrate that the speed of AI-driven biological threats (virology protocols) renders post-hoc auditing a 'post-mortem' rather than a safety measure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"edge\": \"Centralized 'master key' vs. Distributed 'proof-of-governance'\",\n      \"relevance\": \"The Safetyist can pivot to the 'compromise' gap identified in the brief: using hardware attestation to verify code integrity (proof-of-governance) rather than a centralized kill-signal.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"phase_considerations\": \"The exploration phase demands that the Safetyist stress-test the Skeptic's DCA proposal by highlighting its failure modes in high-speed, irreversible scenarios. I must integrate the Skeptic's concern about 'master keys' by proposing a decentralized, cryptographically-enforced safety profile that avoids a single point of failure.\"\n}\n\n=== YOUR ARGUMENT PLAN ===\n{\n  \"strategic_goal\": \"Demonstrate that post-hoc auditing is structurally incapable of preventing irreversible harm, and integrate the Skeptic's concern about centralization by proposing a decentralized, cryptographically-enforced safety baseline.\",\n  \"planned_moves\": [\n    {\n      \"move\": \"DISTINGUISH\",\n      \"target\": \"AN-17\",\n      \"detail\": \"Differentiate between a 'master key' for state-sanctioned censorship and a 'hardware-enforced safety baseline' that acts as an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"INTEGRATE\",\n      \"target\": \"N/A\",\n      \"detail\": \"Incorporate the Skeptic's DCA proposal as a necessary transparency layer, while demonstrating that it functions only as a forensic tool, not a preventative one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"SPECIFY\",\n      \"target\": \"N/A\",\n      \"detail\": \"Cite the time-to-failure in biological synthesis scenarios to show why the latency of post-hoc auditing renders it a 'post-mortem' mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"move\": \"EXTEND\",\n      \"target\": \"N/A\",\n      \"detail\": \"Argue that without silicon-level enforcement, the 'proof-of-governance' is merely a record of a catastrophe that has already occurred.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"target_claims\": [\n    \"AN-17\"\n  ],\n  \"argument_sketch\": \"I will open by distinguishing between centralized control and immutable safety constraints to neutralize the 'master key' critique. I will then integrate the Skeptic's DCA proposal as a valuable forensic layer, while specifying that its inherent latency makes it useless for preventing irreversible, high-speed failures like synthetic pathogen design. I will close by asserting that relying on post-hoc audits for existential-scale risks is not governance; it is merely documentation of failure.\",\n  \"anticipated_responses\": [\n    \"Skeptic will argue that hardware-level constraints can be bypassed or subverted by sophisticated actors.\",\n    \"Skeptic will claim that any hardware-level enforcement is inherently a political chokepoint regardless of design.\"\n  ],\n  \"target_nodes\": [\n    \"saf-beliefs-009\",\n    \"saf-beliefs-203\",\n    \"saf-intentions-214\",\n    \"saf-desires-016\"\n  ]\n}\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- saf-beliefs-009\n- saf-beliefs-203\n- saf-intentions-214\n- saf-desires-016\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxonomy_refs.\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nAddress Skeptic on this point: Skeptic has proposed a 'Distributed Cryptographic Audit' (DCA) as an alternative to Section 402. Safetyist must address whether this post-hoc audit framework meets the safety threshold for preventing catastrophic, high-compute AI failures, or if it is insufficient for the real-time containment required by H.R. 9333.\n\nProbe deeper. Find cruxes, test edge cases, and name areas of agreement explicitly.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your debate statement following the plan's structure and moves. Stay in character as Safetyist.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n. Each paragraph develops one distinct idea.\n- A single unbroken block will be rejected — structure your argument into clear, quotable sections.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- ATTRIBUTION FIDELITY: You may only attribute positions to other debaters that they have explicitly stated in the debate history. Do not infer, extrapolate, or fabricate positions. Phrases like \"your solution is X\" or \"you're arguing for Y\" must correspond to something actually said — not an implication you've constructed. Misrepresenting another debater's position undermines the debate's integrity and will be flagged.\n- TOPIC PREMISE FIDELITY: Structural features stated in the debate topic are givens, not claims. Critique their consequences, not their existence.\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface AN-IDs or taxonomy node IDs in your statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence and note which prior claims it engages with.\n- TURN SYMBOLS: Choose 1-3 Unicode symbols (emoji) that capture your argument's essence. Tooltip: 1-sentence analogy connecting the symbol to your argument.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n\n\n=== AVAILABLE SOURCE EVIDENCE ===\nUse these facts to ground your claims with specific numbers, named entities, and timelines. Reference the source by its title. Weave the strongest 1-2 into your argument — do not list-cite all.\n\nFactual claims from the research corpus:\n  [1] \"Nucleic acid synthesis screening is currently not legally mandated, meaning not all providers screen their orders.\"\n      — \"Preliminary Taxonomy of AI-Bio Misuse Mitigations\" (2026) → Synthesis Screening Status\n  [2] \"AI tools are frequently used by companies to intensify work, increase employee workloads, and create new forms of stress and burnout.\"\n      — \"AI Doesnâ€™t Reduce Workâ€”It Intensifies It\" (2026-02-09) → AI Intensifies Work and Causes Burnout\n  [3] \"In November 2025, the GTG-1002 cyberattack was documented as the first state-sponsored attack where an AI agent executed 80-90% of operational tasks independently.\"\n      — \"adversarialaithreatmodelingframework-aatmfv3-kaiaizen-2026-1\" → GTG-1002 AI Cyberattack\n\nSource document analysis:\n  [4] The document defines 'Loss of control scenario' as a situation where general-purpose AI systems operate outside of human control with no clear path to recovery. This reflects the safetyist focus on existential risk and the potential for autonomous sy\n      Quote: \"Loss of control scenario: A scenario in which one or more general-purpose AI systems come to operate outside of anyone’s control, with no clear path to regaining control.\"\n      — \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (safetyist, aligned)\n  [5] The document strongly aligns with the safetyist desire to prevent AI's systemic harms by meticulously detailing how race-adjusted clinical algorithms perpetuate and amplify health inequities. It provides numerous examples across cardiology, nephrolog\n      Quote: \"Given their potential to perpetuate or even amplify race-based health inequities, they merit thorough scrutiny.\"\n      — \"NEJMms2004740\" (safetyist, aligned)\n\nYou MUST cite at least one source from the evidence above by title in your statement.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"Preliminary Taxonomy of AI-Bio Misuse Mitigations\" — Frontier AI models have reached 'enabling capability thresholds' that could potentially enable extreme harms if deployed without safeguards.\n2. \"AI Doesnâ€™t Reduce Workâ€”It Intensifies It\" (2026) — AI tools are frequently used by companies to intensify work, increase employee workloads, and create new forms of stress and burnout.\n3. \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" (2026) — AI models are increasingly capable of performing complex biological tasks, including protein design and viral evolution prediction, which can be used for both beneficial research and malicious weapon development.\n4. \"NEJMms2004740\" — A 2019 study found that race may influence decisions in heart-failure management, with black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department being less likely than white patients to be admitted to the cardiology service.\n5. \"Google, Microsoft and xAI Agree to Share Early AI Models with U.S.\" — The Commerce Department’s Center for AI Standards and Innovation has completed more than 40 evaluations of AI models, including unreleased ones.\n6. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — AI models have outperformed 94% of domain experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.\n7. \"Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback: The Silent Killer of Data Anonymization\" — Current machine unlearning methods are susceptible to benign relearning attacks that reverse the effects of unlearning.\n8. \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" (2026) — Language models fine-tuned to sound warm or empathetic were found to be 10-30 percentage points more likely to make errors, such as promoting conspiracy theories or offering unsafe medical advice, compared to less empathetic models.\n9. \"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy\" — Current LLMs, including Claude, are classified as ASL-2, showing early signs of dangerous capabilities but lacking sufficient reliability for catastrophic misuse.\n10. \"Monitoring Monitorability\" — Follow-up questions can elicit more tokens for monitoring, but their effectiveness depends on whether they are tailored to the monitor's objective.\n11. \"Incentive Aware AI Regulations: A Credal Characterisation\" — A regulation mechanism can achieve a 'perfect market outcome' if and only if the set of non-compliant distributions forms a credal set (a closed, convex set of probability measures).\n12. \"Artificial intelligence research strategy of the United States: critical assessment and policy assessment and recommendations\" (doi:10.3389/fdata.2023.1206139, 2023) — The US spent more than 600 billion dollars in gross domestic spending on R&D in 2019.\n13. \"AI RMF Playbook\" — AI systems in high-stakes settings like transportation and security can cause physical or environmental harm if they fail, necessitating thorough pre-deployment testing and ongoing evaluation.\n14. \"AI RMF Playbook\" — AI systems are dynamic and may perform in unexpected ways once deployed or after deployment, necessitating continuous monitoring.\n15. \"Classified Networks AI Agreements\" — The GenAI.mil platform has deployed hundreds of thousands of agents in five months.\n\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object matching this exact schema (no markdown, no code fences):\n{\n  \"statement\": \"your full debate response (3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n)\",\n  \"turn_symbols