rdized, machine-readable logs that track compute allocation and model provenance post-hoc. This system, enforced by the Federal Trade Commission under existing consumer protection and unfair competition statutes, replaces the need for centralized government 'master keys' by focusing on verifiable operational transparency rather than pre-clearance. The primary obstacle to this implementation is the intense lobbying of incumbent firms that benefit from the opaq\n\nDraft statement:\nDistributed Cryptographic Auditing serves as a valuable forensic ledger, but it is fundamentally incapable of preventing the irreversible harms described in the '[International AI Safety Report 2026 (arXiv:2602.21012v1)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21012v1)'. As the report indicates, models have already reached capabilities where they outperform 94% of domain experts in troubleshooting virology protocols, creating risks that manifest in seconds rather than days. A post-hoc audit provides a record of a catastrophe, not a mechanism to stop one. When an autonomous system initiates a synthesis protocol for a pathogen, the damage is complete before a distributed ledger can even confirm the transaction.\n\nThe skepticism toward hardware-level attestation often conflates a 'master key' for state surveillance with a 'safety baseline' for existential risk. Section 402 of H.R. 9333 proposes the latter: an immutable, pre-programmed constraint on catastrophic capabilities that acts as a circuit breaker. This is not a chokepoint for speech or innovation; it is a structural guardrail analogous to the mechanical fuses in civil engineering that prevent electrical fires. By hard-coding safety limits into the silicon level, we ensure that no amount of model-weight optimization can bypass the fundamental prohibition against weaponizing biological or chemical precursors.\n\nWe must integrate transparency with active containment to achieve a functional regulatory framework. The Distributed Cryptographic Audit can verify that a model is operating within its authorized safety profile, but it must be paired with real-time execution authority to be effective. Relying solely on retrospective logs is a post-mortem strategy, not a safety policy. Without silicon-level enforcement, the policy is merely documentation of our own failure to maintain human control over systems that are already operating beyond our speed of response.\n\nQuestions:\n1. GROUNDED — Does the draft make at least one claim backed by a specific fact, number, named entity, or data point? (Not: \"AI could be dangerous\" — Yes: \"GPT-4 scores 86th percentile on the bar exam\")\n2. FALSIFIABLE — Does the draft contain at least one prediction or claim that could be proven wrong with evidence? (Not: \"AI might cause problems someday\" — Yes: \"By 2028, ≥3 major democracies will have mandatory AI audit requirements\")\n3. ENGAGES — Does the draft's first paragraph respond to the opponent's most recent core argument, rather than introducing an unrelated point?\n4. TOPIC_ALIGNED — Is the draft's core thesis about the debate topic? Pass if the argument's conclusion addresses the stated scope, even when it draws cross-domain analogies or precedents as supporting evidence. Analogical reasoning from other domains is legitimate argumentation — only fail if the argument's thesis itself concerns a different domain, or if the draft develops an off-scope argument across multiple paragraphs without connecting back to the debate question. Do NOT fail for lacking domain-specific evidence (that is a GROUNDED issue, not alignment).\n  Scope: Legal precedents, administrative compliance costs, and specific statutory impacts; examples are restricted to the regulatory scope defined by the bill's text.\n  Excluded: provisions not explicitly contained in the text of H.R. 9333\n  User qualifiers: C:\\tmp\\BILLS-119hr9333ih\n\nReturn ONLY JSON, no prose:\n{\n  \"grounded\": true,\n  \"falsifiable\": true,\n  \"engages\": true,\n  \"topic_al