-Model Decoupling: A situation concept that describes the divergence between legally binding acceptable use policies and the actual technical capabilities or enforcement mechanisms of a model. Encompasses: unenforceable policy clauses, gaps between model cards and AUPs, and reliance on third-party distribution.\n  Your interpretation: Safetyists would interpret this as a dangerous gap that allows developers to claim safety compliance while failing to implement robust, verifiable, and enforceable safeguards.\n  Accelerationist: Accelerationists might view this as a necessary evolution where legal frameworks lag behind rapid technical deployment, suggesting that over-regulation could stifle the beneficial uses of these models.\n  Skeptic: Skeptics would see this as evidence of 'safety washing,' where legal policies are used as a marketing or liability-sh...\n\n=== POLICY ACTIONS (reference by pol-NNN ID when relevant) ===\nThese are concrete policy actions identified in the research. When your argument supports or opposes a specific policy, reference its ID in your policy_refs.\n(★ = most relevant to current topic)\n★ [pol-001] Fund retraining and reskilling programs for workers in sectors vulnerable to AI automation. (situations, skeptic)\n★ [pol-006] Implement taxation on AI-generated profits and automated labor for public benefit. (situations, skeptic)\n★ [pol-028] Fund research and development for AI safety and risk mitigation. (accelerationist, safetyist, situations, skeptic)\n  [pol-035] Mandate transparency for AI system data sources and methodologies. (situations)\n  [pol-093] Fund research into AI model interpretability and internal workings understanding (safetyist, situations, skeptic)\n  [pol-1000] Invest in public education and training programs to enable the general population to effectively utilize advanced AI capabilities. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1002] Grant temporary waivers for specific AI projects from certain existing regulations. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1003] Invest in education and retraining programs for future AI-driven job markets. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1004] Avoid policies that restrict AI development based on job displacement concerns. (accelerationist)\n  [pol-1007] Reform copyright law to establish fair use exemptions for AI training data. (accelerationist)\n=== VOCABULARY CONSTRAINTS ===\nUse only standardized terms from the vocabulary below. Do not use bare colloquial terms — they are ambiguous across camps and the system will reject them. The display form will be substituted for the canonical form when output is shown to readers.\n\nYour camp's terms (safetyist):\n  accountability_institutional → \"accountability (institutional)\": Formal legal, regulatory, and organizational structures that assign liability, mandate disclosure, and provide remedi...\n  autonomy_human → \"autonomy (human preservation)\": The principle that AI systems must not undermine meaningful human decision-making authority over consequential life c...\n  capabilities_hazard → \"capabilities (hazard)\": The danger posed by increasing AI capability: as systems become more capable, they become harder to control, align, a...\n  control_human_agency → \"control (human agency)\": Preserving meaningful human decision-making authority over consequential choices, even as AI systems become more capa...\n  deployment_gated → \"deployment (safety-gated)\": AI system deployment conditional on passing pre-deployment safety evaluation, verification, or certification before m...\n  fairness_group → \"fairness (group/demographic)\": AI systems should produce equitable outcomes across demographic groups — equal error rates, equal positive/negative p...\n  governance_oversight → \"governance (oversight)\": External institutional frameworks that constrain, monitor, and regulate AI development and deployment. Governance as ...\n  liability_strict → \"liability (strict/product)\": Legal regime holding AI developers strictly liable for harm