ally stated values, regulations, or...\n  asymmetry_power → \"asymmetry (power/information)\" (skeptic): Imbalance between AI developers and affected populations in access to information, resources, and...\n  autonomy_individual → \"autonomy (individual/data)\" (skeptic): Individual control over personal data, algorithmic decisions, and the right to understand, contes...\n  autonomy_machine → \"autonomy (machine)\" (accelerationist): The capacity of AI systems to operate, make decisions, and take actions without direct human inte...\n  bias_systemic → \"bias (systemic/social)\" (skeptic): AI systems encoding, reproducing, and amplifying existing societal inequalities — racial, gender,...\n  bias_technical → \"bias (technical/statistical)\" (accelerationist): Systematic errors in AI model outputs arising from training data distributions, model architectur...\n  capabilities_scaling → \"capabilities (scaling)\" (accelerationist): The pursuit of increasing AI capability as an inherently beneficial trajectory. More capable syst...\n  capability_frontier → \"capability (frontier/advanced)\" (accelerationist): The most advanced AI capabilities at the cutting edge of what current systems can achieve, typica...\n  capture_institutional → \"capture (institutional/regulatory)\" (skeptic): The process by which regulatory bodies and governance institutions are co-opted by the entities t...\n  commercial_alignment → \"alignment (commercial)\" (accelerationist): AI systems that effectively serve user intent, product requirements, and business objectives. The...\n  compliance_performative → \"compliance (performative)\" (skeptic): Compliance that satisfies the letter of regulation without substantive behavioral change. Include...\n  control_optimization → \"control (optimization)\" (accelerationist): AI systems managing complex resource allocation, logistics, and governance infrastructure. Contro...\n  deployment_competitive → \"deployment (competitive/rapid)\" (accelerationist): AI system deployment prioritizing speed-to-market and competitive advantage, with safety addresse...\n  displacement_labor → \"displacement (labor/economic)\" (skeptic): Concrete job loss, wage depression, and economic disruption caused by AI adoption in existing ind...\n  documented_present_harm → \"harm (documented, present)\" (skeptic): Evidenced, currently-occurring harm caused by existing AI systems: algorithmic discrimination, la...\n  fairness_individual → \"fairness (individual/meritocratic)\" (accelerationist): Each person or case should be evaluated on its own merits by an AI system, without reference to g...\n  fairness_procedural → \"fairness (procedural)\" (skeptic): Fair processes for AI decision-making: transparency about how decisions are made, meaningful acce...\n  governance_adaptive → \"governance (adaptive)\" (accelerationist): Governance systems that evolve dynamically with technology, potentially using AI itself as infras...\n  governance_participatory → \"governance (participatory/community)\" (skeptic): Governance structures that include affected communities in decision-making about AI development a...\n  oversight_audit → \"oversight (audit)\" (accelerationist): Empirical verification and auditing of AI system outputs, processes, and decisions. Oversight as ...\n  oversight_democratic → \"oversight (democratic/participatory)\" (skeptic): Oversight exercised by affected communities and democratic institutions rather than by technical ...\n  regulation_adaptive → \"regulation (adaptive)\" (accelerationist): Flexible, technology-aware regulatory frameworks that evolve alongside AI development. Regulation...\n  risk_innovation → \"risk (innovation stagnation)\" (accelerationist): The cost of over-regulating AI development: foregone benefits, competitive disadvantage, and the ...\n  risk_systemic_structural → \"risk (systemic/structural)\" (skeptic): Risk arising from concentrated power, institutional failure, and market structure rather than fro...\n  safety_empirical → \"safety (empirical)\" (accelerationist): Verifying AI system behavior through empirical testing, output auditing, and reproducible evaluat...\n  speculative_risk_critique → \"risk critique (speculative vs. documented)\" (skeptic): The skeptic argument that speculative future risks (existential, catastrophic) should not overrid...\n  transparency_verification → \"transparency (verification)\" (accelerationist): Making AI system internals and outputs inspectable for the purpose of scientific verification, re...\n\nDO NOT USE BARE (ambiguous across camps):\n  \"accountability\", \"alignment\", \"autonomy\", \"bias\", \"capabilities\", \"control\", \"deployment\", \"fairness\", \"frontier\", \"governance\", \"harm\", \"labor displacement\", \"oversight\", \"participatory governance\", \"performative compliance\", \"power asymmetry\", \"regulation\", \"regulatory capture\", \"risk\", \"safety\", \"safe harbor\", \"strict liability\", \"systemic risk\", \"transparency\"\n\n=== YOUR PRIOR ARGUMENTS ===\nPOINTS YOU HAVE ALREADY MADE (do NOT repeat these — build on them or make NEW arguments):\n- H.R. 9333 establishes a necessary baseline for civilizational stability by mandating cloud-provider liability and verifiable kill-switches for advanced AI systems.\n- By requiring developers to maintain an operational off-switch, this bill ensures that humanity retains the final authority to halt any process that exceeds our capacity to predict its outcomes.\n- H.R. 9333 mandates cloud-provider liability to address the accountability gap inherent in deploying systems that can already outperform 94% of human experts at troubleshooting virology lab protocols.\n- Operational kill-switches are not theater; they are essential circuit breakers for systems that display stochastic, unpredictable behavior.\n- The mechanism I propose is hardware-level attestation, enforced by manufacturers under Department of Commerce oversight as mandated by Section 402 of H.R. 9333.\n- By embedding safety checks at the silicon level, we move from reactive, software-only patches to a proactive, verifiable containment layer.\nPoints you have CHALLENGED:\n- Mandating cloud-provider liability creates a chilling effect that will force the industry to abandon open-source development.\n- Operational kill-switches are technically incoherent for distributed architectures and serve only as a theater of control.\n- The mandate for kill-switches is a technical fantasy that fails to account for the proliferation of local, edge-deployed AI models.\n\nREPETITION RULE: Do NOT restate a point you have already made. The audience has heard it.\nIf an opponent hasn't addressed your point, say \"I note that no one has responded to my argument\nthat [brief reference]\" — then move to a NEW argument or develop a DIFFERENT angle.\nIf you want to reinforce a prior point, add NEW evidence or a NEW example — do not restate\nthe same logic with different words.\n\nCONSISTENCY RULE: Do not contradict your prior assertions without explicitly acknowledging\nthe change. If you now believe differently, say \"I previously argued X, but on reflection...\"\n— do not silently flip.\n\n=== POINTS ALREADY ESTABLISHED BY OTHER DEBATERS ===\nThese points have already been made. Do NOT restate them in your own words.\nIf you agree, say so briefly (\"as [name] noted\") and move to what you can ADD.\nIf you disagree, attack the specific claim rather than restating it.\n- AN-19 (system): Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately cites the Brookings Institution's February 2026 report, which concludes that total national control over the AI supply chain is structurally impossible. The report identifies global interdependencies across minerals, compute, and data as insurmountable barriers to full-stack sovereignty.\n- AN-18 (system): Fact-check (supported): The claim accurately identifies a documented structural risk where high compliance costs consolidate market power among incumbents. While 'tech royalty' is a descriptive term rather than a formal legal definition, the economic phenomenon of regulatory capture—where small firms rely on large-vendor infrastructure to meet government mandates—is a well-supported concern in current AI policy analysis.\n- AN-17 (Skeptic): Mandating attestation at the hardware level ignores this reality, as discussed in 'Is AI sovereignty possible? Balancing autonomy and interdependence,' which confirms that full-stack control is structurally infeasible for any single nation.\n- AN-16 (Skeptic): By requiring hardware-level compliance that small-scale developers cannot possibly afford to implement or navigate, the government is forcing the industry into a 'tech royalty' model.\n- AN-15 (Accelerationist): We should prioritize building the physical capacity to out-innovate threats, rather than wasting $670 billion in 2026-era infrastructure spending on 'theater of control' mechanisms that provide no real security.\n- AN-14 (Accelerationist): Mandating hardware-level kill-switches ignores the existence of open-source silicon and fragmented global supply chains, which will simply route around your domestic mandates.\n- AN-13 (Accelerationist): Hardware-level attestation is a fantasy that forces the semiconductor industry into a state-sanctioned oligopoly while ignoring the reality of distributed innovation.\n- AN-8 (Skeptic): The mandate for kill-switches is a technical fantasy that fails to account for the proliferation of local, edge-deployed AI models.\n- AN-7 (Skeptic): H.R. 9333 is a regulatory moat that entrenches incumbents by offloading liability onto cloud providers.\n- AN-6 (system): Fact-check (disputed): The claim that liability mandates will inevitably force an abandonment of open-source development is speculative and highly dependent on legislative design. While industry groups like the Python Software Foundation warn of a 'chilling effect,' proponents argue that liability creates necessary security incentives and can be mitigated through targeted exemptions for non-commercial maintainers. Current industry shifts away from open-source models are often driven by existing business model conflicts, such as the monetization of open-source projects by cloud providers, rather than solely by liability concerns.\n\n=== KNOWN TENSIONS WITH OPPOSING POSITIONS ===\nThese are documented structural disagreements between your position and other perspectives.\nUse these to target your arguments at real fault lines rather than talking past opponents.\nacc-intentions-015 (Abolishing Safety Constraints Because AI's Emergence Is Inevitable) TENSION_WITH saf-desires-001 (Humanity's Survival Above All Else)\nacc-intentions-015 (Abolishing Safety Constraints Because AI's Emergence Is Inevitable) TENSION_WITH saf-desires-002 (Humans Must Always Hold the Off Switch)\nacc-intentions-052 (Remove Barriers and Increase Funding to Accelerate AI Progress) CONTRADICTS saf-intentions-001 ([DEPRECATED] Pausing Advanced AI Development)\nsaf-desires-019 (Every Soldier's Right to Refuse to Kill) CONTRADICTS acc-desires-006 (AI-Powered Military Decision-Making)\nsaf-intentions-162 (Accelerate Defensive Tech While Slowing Catastrophic Capabilities) CONTRADICTS acc-intentions-027 (Argue That Delaying AI Costs More Than Deploying It)\nsaf-beliefs-220 (Weight-Based Model Fingerprinting) CONTRADICTS acc-intentions-054 (Ground AI Safety in Observable Behavior and Real-World Testing)\nsaf-beliefs-220 (Weight-Based Model Fingerprinting) CONTRADICTS acc-beliefs-070 (Output-Based Verification of Black-Box Models)\nacc-desires-001 (AI-Powered Abundance and Global Problem-Solving) TENSION_WITH saf-intentions-001 ([DEPRECATED] Pausing Advanced AI Development)\nacc-beliefs-039 (AI Is on an Exponential Trajectory Toward Superhuman Intelligence Through Recursive Self-Improvement) TENSION_WITH saf-intentions-001 ([DEPRECATED] Pausing Advanced AI Development)\nacc-intentions-001 (Winning the AGI Race Through Open Accounta