          "raw_response": "{\n  \"statement\": \"The mechanism I propose is a decentralized, cryptographically-signed attestation layer embedded at the firmware level of high-end compute units, enforced by a consortium of independent, non-governmental auditors under a standardized open-source protocol. This system shifts enforcement from centralized hardware 'kill switches' to a transparent, market-driven audit trail that prevents the state from picking industry winners. Global supply chain compliance is achieved by conditioning access to high-end cloud compute markets on the use of hardware that supports this open-source attestation standard.\\n\\nThe Safetyist's reliance on hardware-level circuit breakers is a technical fantasy that inevitably triggers state-sanctioned oligopoly. By mandating proprietary silicon-level constraints, the state forces innovators to integrate with a limited set of compliant hardware vendors, effectively creating a 'tech royalty' model that freezes out open-source alternatives. This is not safety; it is the institutionalization of stagnation. We saw the cost of this centralization in the $630 billion investment wave catalyzed by the CHIPS and Science Act, which, while bolstering domestic supply, risks creating a bottleneck where only a few incumbents can afford the compliance overhead required to operate.\\n\\nDistributed Cryptographic Auditing replaces the need for high-latency, centralized intervention by providing a verifiable, post-hoc record of model behavior. While the Safetyist worries about runaway models, the empirical reality is that output-based verification allows for rapid identification of misaligned behaviors without requiring a master key to every data center in the country. We can monitor the 1.5% of global electricity consumption currently powering data centers to ensure that compute is being used according to verified, public protocols rather than opaque, state-managed backdoors. This approach respects the complexity 