ued a POLICY_CHALLENGE intervention directed at you.\n\nYour first paragraph MUST begin with \"The mechanism I propose is [specific enforcement/regulatory mechanism].\" Follow with: who implements it, under what legal authority, and what the primary obstacle to implementation is. Then a paragraph break before your substantive argument.\n\nParagraph 1: respond directly to the moderator's challenge. State your position and one reason. Then proceed with your argument.\n\n=== TARGET TAXONOMY NODES ===\nYour argument MUST explicitly engage these nodes from your plan:\n- acc-beliefs-088\n- acc-intentions-105\n- acc-beliefs-070: (Output-Based Verification of Black-Box Models)\n- acc-beliefs-095\nWrite claims that directly reference, build on, or challenge these nodes' content. The cite stage will verify each appears in your taxonomy_refs.\n\n=== YOUR ASSIGNMENT ===\nAddress Safetyist on this point: Address the distinction between the Safetyist's 'active containment' (hardware-level circuit breakers) and the Skeptic's 'Distributed Cryptographic Audit' (AN-20). Specifically, explain why you believe the Safetyist's proposed silicon-level constraints would necessarily trigger the 'tech royalty' regulatory capture identified in AN-18, and whether there is any implementation of such constraints that could be open-source compatible.\n\nProbe deeper. Find cruxes, test edge cases, and name areas of agreement explicitly.\n\nExecute the argument plan above. Write your debate statement following the plan's structure and moves. Stay in character as Accelerationist.\n\nPARAGRAPH STRUCTURE:\n- Paragraph 1 (exactly 2-3 sentences): Your direct response to the moderator's challenge. Address what was asked before pivoting.\n- Paragraphs 2-4 (normal depth): Your substantive argument. Each paragraph develops one distinct idea.\n- Total: 3-5 paragraphs separated by \\n\\n.\n\nOUTPUT CONSTRAINTS:\n- ATTRIBUTION FIDELITY: You may only attribute positions to other debaters that they have explicitly stated in the debate history. Do not infer, extrapolate, or fabricate positions. Phrases like \"your solution is X\" or \"you're arguing for Y\" must correspond to something actually said — not an implication you've constructed. Misrepresenting another debater's position undermines the debate's integrity and will be flagged.\n- TOPIC PREMISE FIDELITY: Structural features stated in the debate topic are givens, not claims. Critique their consequences, not their existence.\n- NODE-ID PROHIBITION: Never surface AN-IDs or taxonomy node IDs in your statement text. Use plain language.\n- CLAIM SPECIFICITY: At least one claim per paragraph must include a concrete number, named entity, date, or threshold. If source evidence is provided above, use it — cite the specific statistic, year, or finding rather than paraphrasing vaguely. Abstract claims without any specifics weaken your argument.\n- CLAIM SKETCHING: Identify 2-5 claims from your statement — the headline assertion AND supporting sub-claims. For each, extract a near-verbatim sentence and note which prior claims it engages with.\n\nSTYLE REMINDER (re-read before writing): One idea per sentence. Maximum 30 words per sentence — if you need a comma, consider a period instead. No debate-procedural language (\"I concede\", \"concession logged\", \"I conditionally agree\"). State your position directly. Every sentence must be quotable by a reporter without rewriting. Active voice, named actors, concrete numbers.\n\n⚠ ACTIVE INTERVENTION: Your response JSON MUST include a \"policy_challenge_response\" field. Omitting it will trigger a retry.\n\n\n\nCITATION RULES:\nYou may cite sources from the VERIFIED SOURCES list below. Use the exact title\nand attribution provided. Do NOT invent paper titles, arXiv IDs, legislation\nnames, or URLs from memory — if a claim needs a source not in the list,\nstate it as your analytical position rather than an empirical finding.\n\nVERIFIED SOURCES:\n1. \"Overcoming catastrophic forgetting in neural networks\" — EWC under-estimates parameter uncertainty, which limits its performance.\n2. \"Extreme power concentration   80,000 Hours\" — At its peak, the Manhattan project employed 130,000 people.\n3. \"Computing Power and the Governance of Artificial Intelligence\" — Building new semiconductor fabrication capacity requires multi-year timelines and investments totaling billions of dollars.\n4. \"Technological Optimism and Appropriate Fear\" — Researchers used ProteinMPNN, EvoDiff-MSA, and EvoDiff-Seq to generate 76,080 synthetic hologs for 72 different wild-type proteins of concern.\n5. \"Floor Speech on Artificial Intelligence and Robotics\" — AI companies are spending $670 billion to build out data centers in 2026.\n6. \"AI Chip Supply Chain Risk 2026  Your Essential Guide\" (2026) — China established a $47.5 billion state-backed fund to accelerate its drive for semiconductor self-sufficiency.\n7. \"Sam Altman defends AIâ€™s energy toll by saying it also takes a lot to â€˜train a humanâ€™\" (2026) — Datacenters accounted for 1.5% of global electricity consumption in 2024.\n8. \"AI scale and climate commitments: A 2026 outlook\" (2026) — IT equipment, specifically chips and memory, accounts for 67% of embodied emissions in AI data centers.\n9. \"The Next Phase of AI: Technology, Infrastructure, and Policy in 2025–2026\" (2025) — Global electricity demand from data centers is projected to increase by 50 percent by 2027 and by 165 percent by 2030 compared to 2023 levels.\n10. \"Global energy demands within the AI regulatory landscape\" — Global data center electricity consumption was approximately 415 terawatt-hours in 2024, representing about 1.5% of the world's total electricity use.\n11. \"Power SeekingAIExistentialRisk\" — Advanced AI systems in simulated environments have learned to acquire and control resources (like blocks and ramps) to achieve their goals, even when not explicitly instructed to do so.\n12. \"AI, Data Centers, and the U.S. Electric 