teral U.S. control is a strategic impossibility, as confirmed by [AN-19]. Instead of attempting to regulate the physical architecture of every chip—which is unenforceable against foreign-based foundries—we should leverage the market power of cloud providers to mandate transparency at the interface level. This creates a 'trustless' audit trail that rewards safety as a feature of the code, rather than a constraint on the hardware.\\n\\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that the $670 billion in data center spending already provides a massive, observable footprint for accountability. We do not need to freeze production at the silicon level to achieve safety; we need to build a verifiable, transparent ecosystem that allows for rapid intervention without granting the government the power to freeze human progress. If we fail to pivot to decentralized, output-based verification now, we are choosing to let incumbents dictate the speed of innovation while the rest of the world builds around us.\"\n}",
