em.\"\n\nMANDATORY CORRECTION — ADD SPECIFICS:\nThe rejected draft lacked concrete details. Include at least one: a specific number (\"≥20%\"), a named entity (\"the EU AI Act\"), or a timeline (\"by 2028\").\n\n\n=== SITUATION BRIEF ===\n{\n  \"situation_assessment\": \"The debate has reached a stalemate between the Safetyist's demand for hardware-level 'circuit breakers' and the Accelerationist's push for decentralized, post-hoc cryptographic auditing. The moderator has pushed both sides to move beyond abstract principles and address the practical implementation challenges of global supply chain compliance and the risk of regulatory capture.\",\n  \"key_claims_to_address\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Hardware-level circuit breakers create a path to state-sanctioned stagnation and regulatory capture by incumbents.\",\n      \"speaker\": \"Accelerationist\",\n      \"an_id\": \"AN-13\",\n      \"grounding\": [\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"saf-intentions-211\",\n          \"label\": \"Compliance Standards and Enforcement Mechanisms\",\n          \"operationality\": 5,\n          \"why\": \"Primary — addresses the vulnerability of compliance standards to incumbent capture.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"cc-137\",\n          \"label\": \"Regulatory Credibility Erosion\",\n          \"confidence\": 0.75,\n          \"why\": \"Supporting — highlights the risk of creating unenforceable or market-distorting mandates.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"saf-desires-023\",\n          \"label\": \"Dissent on AI Safety Must Be Protected, Not Punished\",\n          \"priority\": 4,\n          \"why\": \"Supporting — frames the need to avoid chilling effects on smaller, non-incumbent innovators.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Full-stack sovereignty and control over the global semiconductor supply chain is structurally infeasible.\",\n      \"speaker\": \"Skeptic\",\n      \"an_id\": \"AN-17\",\n      \"grounding\": [\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"sit-032\",\n          \"label\": \"Sovereign AI as Strategic Independence\",\n          \"confidence\": 0.7,\n          \"why\": \"Primary — directly addresses the feasibility of the Safetyist's control-based strategy.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"node_id\": \"saf-intentions-069\",\n          \"label\": \"Grounding AI Safety in Legal Compliance Rather Than Abstract Ethics\",\n          \"operationality\": 6,\n          \"why\": \"Supporting — highlights the difficulty of applying domestic legal mandates to globalized hardware.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relevant_commitments\": [\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Safetyist\",\n      \"commitment\": \"Hardware-level attestation is a necessary circuit breaker for catastrophic AI risks.\",\n      \"type\": \"asserted\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"speaker\": \"Accelerationist\",\n      \"commitment\": \"Decentralized cryptographic verification can achieve safety without centralized master keys.\",\n      \"type\": \"asserted\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"edge_tensions\": [\n    {\n      \"edge\": \"The tension between 'safety-gated' deployment and the reality of globalized, open-source hardware.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Safetyist can exploit this by arguing that without a common hardware baseline, 'decentralized' audits are easily spoofed by malicious actors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"edge\": \"The 'regulatory-tech' gap: the potential for automated compliance to lower, rather than raise, barriers to entry.\",\n      \"relevance\": \"Safetyist can pivot to this to counter the 'regulatory capture' argument by proposing that standardized hardware attestation could be an open-source, low-cost utility.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"phase_considerations\": \"The current phase demands a shift from ideological posturing to technical pragmatism. Safetyist must concede the difficulty of total supply chain control while arguing that a 'minimum viable safety baseline' at the hardware level is