              "prompt": "You are a grounding analyst. Your task is to annotate a debate statement with precise taxonomy references, policy connections, and dialectical move annotations.\n\n=== DRAFT STATEMENT ===\n{\n  \"statement\": \"Hardware-level attestation is a fantasy that ignores the reality of global supply chains where seven firms already capture 35% of the S&P 500 market value. According to \\\"Transparency in AI is on the decline,\\\" these companies provide zero disclosure on environmental impact, yet you expect them to embed a government-mandated kill-switch into silicon that passes through a dozen countries before it ever reaches a server rack. If you mandate hardware-level attestation, you simply force the entire industry into jurisdictional arbitrage, where non-compliant hardware is manufactured in zones outside your regulatory reach. You aren't building a circuit breaker; you are building a black market for off-grid compute.\\n\\nYour trustless cryptographic audit trail is just a thicker layer of administrative bloat that fails to address the physical exhaustion of our resources. You propose a ledger to verify what these machines are doing, but you ignore that these same machines consume massive amounts of water and electricity to maintain those very ledgers. As noted in \\\"Transparency in AI is on the decline,\\\" major companies refuse to disclose basic carbon and water usage metrics, meaning your audit trail is auditing a ghost in a burning building. You are debating the software's logic while the infrastructure sustaining it is physically opaque and unaccountable.\\n\\nYou both operate under the assumption that a technical fix can solve a power imbalance, but this is a political problem, not a code problem. Treating AI as a public utility—as proposed in \\\"treat-ai-like-public-utility-2026-2\\\"—requires antitrust enforcement, not just more proprietary hardware or encrypted logs. Stop pretending that you can code safety into a machine that is owned by a handful of corporations that treat disclosure as a competitive disadvantage. The question isn't whether your audit can trigger a containment; it's why you believe these firms would ever let you hold the keys to their profit centers.\",\n  \"turn_symbols\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"⛓️\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"A chain represents the fragile, globalized supply chain that breaks whenever you try to force a single, rigid 'kill-switch' into it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"🚰\",\n      \"tooltip\": \"Water represents the ignored, finite physical reality that your cryptographic ledgers are consuming while we argue about code.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"claim_sketches\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"If you mandate hardware-level attestation, you simply force the entire industry into jurisdictional arbitrage.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-22\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Your trustless cryptographic audit trail is just a thicker layer of administrative bloat that fails to address the physical exhaustion of our resources.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-25\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Treating AI as a public utility requires antitrust enforcement, not just more proprietary hardware or encrypted logs.\",\n      \"targets\": [\n        \"AN-22\",\n        \"AN-25\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"key_assumptions\": [\n    {\n      \"assumption\": \"Hardware-level attestation and cryptographic audits are inherently incompatible with the current globalized, opaque corporate supply chain.\",\n      \"if_wrong\": \"If these technical measures can be successfully implemented despite supply chain complexity, then hardware-level security could provide a viable, enforceable layer of accountability regardless of corporate transparency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"assumption\": \"Technical solutions (code/logs) are fundamentally incapable of addressing power imbalances caused by corporate monopolies.\",\n      \"if_wrong\": \"If